100:
** ἁδροτής**, ῆτος, ἡ,
**vigour, strength**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.31U.}}; of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.4.11}}: _metaphorically_ of sound, **loudness**, Amarant. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.415a}}; of style or expression, **force**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 2.9.}}
**II** **abundance**, [{{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.2Cor.8.20. (In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus variant for{ἀνδροτής}, which see)}}]

101:
** ἀδῠνᾰτ-έω**,
of persons, **to be** ἀδύνατος, **lack strength**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 266, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 454a27}}: with _infinitive_, **to be unable** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.23, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1165b22, “Pol.” 1287b17, etc.}}
**II** of things, **to be impossible**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Job.10.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.98 W., NT.Matt.17.20, NT.Luke.1.37, 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 3.26.}}

102:
** ἀδῠνᾰτ-ος**, ον,
**I** of persons, **unable** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.138, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 272, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 56, etc.; ἀ. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1379a2}}; ἀ. ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.13: _comparative_, τὸν δυνατώτερον τοῦ -ωτέρου [πλέον ἔχειν] 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 483d}}: _superlative_ -ώτατος, λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 95.}}
**I.2** _absolutely_, **without strength, powerless, weakly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.9, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 596, “Andr.” 746}}; οἱ ἀ. men **disabled for service**, whether as **invalids** or **paupers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24 at Title Page, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 49.4; ἐν τοῖς ἀ. μισθοφορεῖν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.103; ἀ. σώματι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.73}}; ἀ. χρήμασι **poor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28}}; εἴς τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 366b}}; οἱ -ώτατοι persons **of no importance**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.8}}; of ships, **disabled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.16}}; τὸ ἀ. **want of strength**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 296a}}; τὰ ἀ. **disabilities**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.108.}}
**II** of things, **impossible**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 665, “Hel.” 1043}}; ἐλπίδες **unrealizable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 58; τὸ ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 280b12; ἡ εἰς τὸ ἀ. ἀπαγωγή}}**reductio ad impossibile, APr.** {{Refs|29b5; ὁ διὰ τοῦ ἀ. συλλογισμός, ἡ διὰ τοῦ ἀ. δεῖξις, [prev. work] 34b30, 45a35; ἀδύνατα βούλομαι Lynceus Comicus 1.12:}} —ἀδύνατόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, +others}}; ἀδύνατά [ἐστι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.81, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, 6.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.59}}; ἀ. ὑμῖν ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338c; ὑμέας καταλελάβηκε ἀ. τι βοηθέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.60; τὰ ἀ. καρτερεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1370; τολμᾶν ἀδύνατα [prev. author] “Hel.” 811; ἀδυνάτων ἐρᾶν [prev. author] “HF” 318, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 8, etc.; proverbial, ἀδύνατα θηρᾷς 15th c.AD: Macarius Paroemiographus 1.26: _comparative_ -ώτερον, ἔτι.. εἰ οἷόν τε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 192b, compare “Prm.” 138d}}: _superlative_, ὃ δὴ πάντων -ώτατον{{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 15b.}}
**III** _adverb_ -τως **without power** or **skill, feebly**, ἀμύνεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.3, compare 3.3.4 (_comparative_), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.3:}} —ἀ. ἔχειν to be **unwell**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 364b}}; to be **unable**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1435a16}}; ἀ. ἔχει it is **impossible**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.49U.}}; ἀ. λέγεται it is an **impossible** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.122 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus —Rare in poetry: Trag. only in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}

103:
** ἀείδω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ form used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}}, and sometimes in Trag. and Comedy texts (even in _trimeter_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 16, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 188; intetram., 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 305)}}, also in _Ionic dialect_ Prose; _contraction_ ᾄδω (also {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 45, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus), Trag., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἤειδον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}}, _Epic dialect_ ἄειδον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, etc.}}; Trag. and _Attic dialect_ ᾖδον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 761, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21}}: _future_ ἀείσομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.352, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 943, but ᾄσομαι “Hymni Homerici” 6.2, 32.19, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 243, and alwaysin _Attic dialect_ (ᾄσεις, σουσιν in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1297, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666d are corrupt)}}; rarely in _active_ form ἀείσω, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 11, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1243 (_Laconian dialect_)}}, and late Poets, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 13.47 (in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 681 ἀείδω is restored by Elmsl.)}}; still more rarely ᾄσω, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 12.13, Men.Rh. p.381S., 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 1.6}}; _Doric dialect_ ᾀσεῦμαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.38, ᾀσῶ [prev. author] 1.145}}: _aorist_ ἤεισα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 23.4, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.1, _Epic dialect_ ἄεισα [ᾰ] 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.411; ἄεισον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 513 (Lyric poetry); ἀείσατε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 115 (Lyric poetry); ᾖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1371, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21b:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἀεισάμην (in _active_ sense) {{Refs|“PMag.Lond.” 47.43, _imperative_ ἀείσεο “Hymni Homerici” 17.1 (nisi to be read ἀείσεο)}}:—_passive_, ἀείδομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}: _poetry_ _imperfect_ ἀείδετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus:}}_aorist_ ᾔσθην, see below 11.1: _perfect_ ᾖσμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 69.11. (ἀϝείδω, compare αὐδή, ὑδέω.) [ᾰ}}: but ᾱ for the sake of meter {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.519, “Hymni Homerici” 12.1, 27.1, “Il.Parv..” 1, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.41, etc.]}}:— **sing**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.604, etc.}}: hence of all kinds of vocal sounds, **crow** as cocks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp..” 223c}} **hoot** as owls, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1000}}; **croak** as frogs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 835b3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 3.5, etc.}}; οἱ τέττιγες χαμόθεν ᾄσονται Stes. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1412a23}}:—of other sounds, **twang**, of the bow-string, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.411}}; **whistle**, of the wind through a tree, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 1.8}}; **ring**, of a stone when struck, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.26:}} —proverbial, πρὶν νενικηκέναι ᾄδειν '**to crow** too soon', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164c.}} —Constr.:—ἀ. τινί **sing to** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.346}}; also, **vie with** one **in singing**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.6}}; ᾄ. πρὸς αὐλὸν ἢ λύραν **sing** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pr..” 918a23; ὑπ᾽ αὐλοῖς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.41c:—ἀείσας.. χαίρειν Δημοκλέα, poetry for εἰπών, “epigram Gr.” 237.7 (from Smyrna).}}
**II** _transitive_,
**II.1** with _accusative_ _of things_, **sing of, chant**, μῆνιν ἄειδε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.1; παιήονα 1.473}}; κλέα ἀνδρῶν, νόστον,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.189, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.326; τὸν Βοιώτιον νόμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 966: with _genitive_ (i.e. μέλος)}}, **sing** an air of.., Φρυνίχου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 269, compare 1225: _absolutely_, ἀ. ἀμφί τινος}} **to sing** in one's praise, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.266; ἀμφί τινα 7th c.BC: Terpander Lyricus 2, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 513; εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1243}}: later, simply ={καλεῖν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.28}}:—_passive_, of songs, **to be sung**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.35; τὰ λεχθέντα καὶ ᾀσέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 205e}}; ᾆσμα καλῶς ᾀσθέν, opposed to λόγος καλῶς ῥηθείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.55}}; ᾄδεται λόγος the story **runs**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.189.}}
**II.2** of persons, places, etc., **sing, praise, celebrate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6.6, etc.:}} —_passive_, ἀείδεται θρέψαισ᾽ ἥρωας **is celebrated** as the nurse of heroes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.25, compare 5.24.}}
**II.3** _passive_, **to be filled with song**, ἀείσετο πᾶν τέμενος.. θαλίαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).76.}}

104:
** ἀεί**,
_adverb_ **ever, always**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; with other specifications of time, ἐμμενὲς αἰεί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.69; συνεχὲς αἰ. 9.74}}; ἀ. καθ᾽ ἡμέραν, καθ᾽ ἡμέραν ἀ., ἀ. καὶ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν, ἀ. κατ᾽ ἐνιαυτόν, ἀ. διὰ βίου, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 75d, etc.; ἀ. πανταχοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.197, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq..” 568; διὰ παντὸς ἀ. “Pax” 397; ἐνδελεχῶς ἀ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 521}}; δεῦρ᾽ ἀεί **until now**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1663, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 811c}}; αἰεί κοτε, ποτε **from of old**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.58, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82; αἰ. δήποτε 1.13}}; compare εἰσαεί:—with the _Article_, ὁ ἀ. χρόνος **eternity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 103e, etc.}}; οἱ ἀ. ὄντες the **immortals**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.46, etc.:}} —but ὁ αἰ. βασιλεύων the king **for the time being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.98; οἱ ἀ. δικάζοντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.223}}; ὁ αἰ. ἐντὸς γιγνόμενος **every one** as he got inside, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.68; τὸν ἀ. προστυχόντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.131}}; τοῖσι τούτων αἰ. ἐκγόνοισι to their descendants **for ever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105, compare 3.83, etc.; in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 937, θῶπτε τὸν κρατοῦντ᾽ ἀ., ἀεί is postponed for the sake of meter—Dialectic forms (cf. Hdn.Gr. 1.497}}, **Et.Gud.z**):
**1** αἰεί, _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, poetical, and Early _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (4th c.AD(?): Marcellinus Biographus “Vita Thucydidis” 52); found (beside ἀεί) in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. to 4th c.BC}}
**2** ἀεί [ᾰ three times in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, ᾱ?~X _Attic dialect_] normal in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. from {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC}}
**3** αἰέν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290, +others (ἆεν is variant in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.827), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 90, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 428, “Ag.” 891, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 682.}}
**4** _Doric dialect_ αἰές, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1266, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 1.1}}; also ἀές, {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.134.}}
**5** _Aeolic dialect_ αἶι(ν), ἄιν, Hdn.Gr.[same place];[{{Refs|compare “IG” 9(2).461 (ἄϊν, Thess.), “SIG” 58 (Milet.), and see at {ἀϊπάρθενος},{ἀείδασμος}.}}]
**6** αἰέ, Hdn.Gr. [prev. cited]
**7** ἀέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.88, Pisander Epicus 11}} (ᾰἐ); compare ἀέ-ναος.
**8** _Boeotian dialect_ ἠί, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. [prev. cited]}}
**9** _Tarentum dialect_ αἰή, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}
**II** τὸ ἀ. **eternity**, τὸ ἀ. τοῦτο οὐκ αἰώνιόν ἐστιν ἀλλὰ χρονικόν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 198. The statement of 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus that ἀεί}} = ἕως in _Attic dialect_ is based on misinterpretation of such phrases as ἐς τόνδε αἰ. τὸν πόλεμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18. (αἰϝεί “epigram Gr.” 742, “GDI” 60.31 (5th c.AD: Cypria), “IG” 9(1).334.4 (_Locrian dialect_)}}, cf. Latin _aevum._)

105:
** ἀετός**,
_Epic dialect_, Lyric poetry, _Ionic dialect_, and early _Attic dialect_ αἰετός (see. near the end), οῦ, ὁ, **eagle**, as a bird of omen, αἰ. τελειότατον πετεηνῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.247, compare 12.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.146 (compare 11)}}: favourite of Zeus, ὅστε σοὶ αὐτῷ φίλτατος οἰωνῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.310, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.6; Διὸς.. πτηνὸς κύων, δαφοινὸς αἰ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1022, compare “Ag.” 136; ὁ σκηπτροβάμων αἰ., κύων Διός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 885:—proverbial, αἰετὸς ἐν ποτανοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.80}}; αἰετὸς ἐν νεφέλαισι, of a thing quite out of reach, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 987}}; ἀετὸν κάνθαρος μαιεύσομαι (see. μαιεύομαι):—the different kinds are distinguished by specific names, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618b18sqq.}}
**2** **eagle as a standard**, of the Persians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.4}}; of the Romans, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 23, etc.}}
**3** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Aquila”, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 591, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 27, etc.}}
**II** **omen**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 26.31.}}
**III** **eagle-ray, Myliobatis aquila**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540b18.}}
**IV** in Architecture, **gable, pediment** (from its resemblance to outspread wings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).519), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1110, ubi see Scholia, “IG” 1.322 ii 80, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.21, “fragment” 53, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 764; ὑπὸ τὸν αὐτὸν ἀετὸν ὑπελθεῖν}}**come under the same roof,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.644 (Bruttii, 3rd c.BC).}}
**V** name of **bandage**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 12.508C.}}
**VI** **temporal vein** (Magna Graecia), Philistionap.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 201.}}
**VII** **iron part of spoke of wheel**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.145, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VIII** Astrology texts and Magic, fabulous plant growing in Libya, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Pamphilus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.798, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.222. (αἰετός}} in early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 1.322ii80, 2.1054.39; αἰητός 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 522, variant in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.4}}; αἰβετός (i.e. αἰϝετός) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus) [ᾱ always.]}}

109:
** ἀήρ**, ἀέρος,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ἀήρ, ἠέρος}}; _Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ ἠήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6, +others, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.3}}; _Aeolic dialect_ αὐήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.52}}; _Doric dialect_ ἀβήρ (i.e. ἀϜήρ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus:— _feminine_ in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus (except “Op.” 549), 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus cited in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 30; from Hdt downwards _masculine_ (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.776, 8.50, “hymnus ad Cererem” 383 cannot be quoted for the _masculine_ usage, since there πουλύς and βαθύς need not be _masculine_):—in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} always **mist, haze**, not (as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus)}} **lower air** (opposed to αἰθήρ, which see); [ἐλάτη] μακροτάτη πεφυυῖα δι᾽ ἠέρος αἰθέρ᾽ ἵκανεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.288, compare 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 264sq.; περὶ δ᾽ ἠέρα πουλὺν ἔχευεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.776, compare 3.381, 8.50; ἠέρα μὲν σκέδας εν καὶ ἀπῶσεν ὀμί χλην 17.649; τρὶς δ᾽ ἠέρα τύψε βαθεῖαν 20.446}}; rare in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus [prev. cited]}}
**2** later, generally, **air**, Anaxim.1, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.18, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 87, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 187, 694, etc.}}; πρὸς τὸν ἀέρα διατρίβειν in **the open air**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 198, compare 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.11.3 H., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 24; τὸν ἀέρ᾽ ἕλκειν καθαρόν 5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 20, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 119; ἔσπασας τὸν ἀ. τὸν κοινόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.7; ἀέρα δέρειν NT.1Cor.9.26; εἰς ἀέρᾳ λαλεῖν [prev. work] 14.9:—in _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98c, 98d}}; **climates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” at Title Page, cf. Men.Rh.p.383 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; of mephitic **exhalations**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.5.}}
**3** personified, ὦ δέσποτ᾽ ἄναξ ἀμέτρητ᾽ Ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 264; Ἀ. ὃν ἄν τις ὀνομάσειε καὶ Δία 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 91.4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 126.6.}}
**II** **hot-air room** in baths, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.14.}}
**II.2** **volume**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Stereom.” 57, +others}}
**III** a pigment, **sky-blue** or **grey**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aut.” 28.3}}. [ᾱ, except in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 642, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea [108].]}}

110:
** ἀθᾰνᾰσία**, ἡ,
**immortality**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1111b22, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.60U., etc.; ὁ δὲ λιμός ἐστιν ἀθανασίας φάρμακον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 86.6.}}
**II** **elixir** or **antidote**, ἀ. Μιθριδάτου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.148 compare 13.203.}}
**II.2** ={ἀμβροσία}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 4.5.}}

111:
** ἀθέμ-ιστος**
or ἀθέμιτος, ον, (the former in Poetry, the latter more correct in Prose) = {ἀθεμίστιος}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.63}}; of the Cyclopes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.106}}; ἀθεμιστότεροι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.5.}}_adverb_ -ίστως Phaennis cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.15.3}} **-ίτως** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 53.}}
**II** of things, **unlawful**, frequently in _neuter_, ἀθέμιτα ἔρδειν{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.33; ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.9; εὔχεσθαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.6; -ιστα δρᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 742 (uncertain), 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator “Fragmenta - Orat.” _Attic dialect_ 89.4S.; κείνοις δ᾽ οὐκ ἀθέμιστον “IG” 14.1389ii29:— ἀ. εἰδωλολατρεῖαι NT.1Pet.4.3: with _dative_, αἷς [θεαῖς] ἀ. νεκρὰ σώματα “PTaur.” 1 ii 22 (2nd c.BC).}}

112:
** ἄθεος**, ον,
**B** **without vision**, τινός{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.17}} (assuming variant; ἀθέ[ατ]ος Volkmann).
**B.2** **unseen**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.10.}}

114:
** ἀθετ-έω**, ἄθετος
) **set at naught** a treaty, promise, etc., πίστιν{{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.36.5; θυσίαν LXX.1Ki.2.17; διαθήκην NT.Gal.3.15; θεόν NT.1Thes. 4.8; σύμφωνον “OGI” 444.18 (from Ilium)}}; **deny, disprove**, τἀληθές{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.5 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare “Sign.” 37 (_passive_)}}:—_passive_, **to be struck off a register,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 74.29 (2nd c.BC)}}; **to be rejected**, of a petition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“POxy.” 1120.8(iii 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus D)}}:—Astrology texts, **cancel, render ineffectual**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 115.3, compare 105.8 (_passive_).}}
**2** with _dative_, **refuse one's assent**, τοῖς ὑπὸ Τιμαίου εἰρημένοις{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.14.6.}}
**3** **deal treacherously with, break faith with**, τινά{{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.36.10, LXX.Isa.1.2, NT.Mark.6.26; εἴς τινα LXX.1Ki.12.19; ἐν Ἰσραήλ LXX.4Ki.1.1: _absolutely_, “IG” 12(5).129 (from Paros).}}
**II** Grammars, **reject as spurious**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 9, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.34, etc.}}
**III** _absolutely_, **to be unsuitable, unfit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

115:
** ἀθέτ-ησις**, ἡ,
**a setting aside, abolition**, ἁμαρτίας{{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Heb.9.26, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.142}}; '**annulling**' of a deed, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.142.24 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **rejection** (of a spurious passage), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 5.8, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.66}}; generally, **rejection**, opposed to ἐποχή, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 6.9.3.}}
**III** **breach of faith**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 191.24 (_plural_).}}

116:
** Ἀθῆναι**,
_Doric dialect_ Ἀθᾶναι, ῶν, αἱ, **the city of Athens** (for the _plural_ compare Θῆβαι, Μυκῆναι), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.; (_sing._ Ἀθήνη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.80, “IG” 1.373107):}} —Ἀθῆναι generally, ={Ἀττική}, of the whole country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.17.}}
**II** _adverbs_, Ἀθήναζε, **to Athens,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 1.27a, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.16}}: Ἀθήνηθεν, **from Athens**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.25, etc.}}; _poetry_ **Ἀθηνόθεν**, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.369 (Antip.)}}: Ἀθήνησιν,
**A** **at Athens,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 1.59, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.66, etc.}}

118:
** ἀθλ-έω**,
_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀέθλεον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67, 7.212:}}_aorist_ ἤθλησα (see. below): _perfect_ ἤθληκα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 5}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐνηθλησάμην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.117 (4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus)}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ κατήθλημαι{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}: (ἆθλος, ἆθλον):—commoner form of {ἀθλεύω}, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_aorist_ _participle_, Λαομέδοντι.. ἀθλήσαντε **having contended with** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.453}}; πολλάπερ ἀθλήσαντα **having gone through** many **struggles**, {{Refs|15.30}}; **contend in battle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.212; πρός τινα 1.67; ἀ. ἄθλους, ἀ. κατὰ τὴν ἀγωνίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 19c and b, compare “Lg.” 830a}}; ἤθλησα κινδυνεύματα **have engaged in** perilous struggles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 564; φαῦλον ἀθλήσας πόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317; ἀ. τῷ σώματι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.147.}}
**II** **to be an athlete, contend** in games, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 149, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2810b (from Aphrodisias).}}
**III** **hold games**, ἐπ᾽ Ἀρχεμόρῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.12.}}

119:
** ἄθλ-ησις**, ἡ,
**contest, combat**, especially of athletes, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.64.6, “SIG” 1073.24 (from Olympia), “IG” 14.1102: _plural_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.14 K.}}; κατὰ τὴν ἄ. 'in the **athletic world',** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|CPHerm. 119 _iii 13 (3rd c.AD)}}; **training, practice**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.33.}}
**2** generally, **struggle, trial**, ἄ. ὑπομένειν{{NT|NT.Heb.10.32.}}

121:
** ἀθῷος**, ον,
(θωά, _Ionic dialect_ θωιή):— **scot-free**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 672, etc.; ἐγὼ μὲν ἀ. ἅπασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.125}}; ἀθῴους καθιστάναι τινάς to secure their **immunity**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.11; ἀθῷον ἀφιέναι Test. cited in [same work] 21.107}}; ἀ. ἀπαλλάττειν or -εσθαι to get off **scot-free**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 254d, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.4; ἀπέρχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 40; διαφυγεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 130.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **free from** a thing, πληγῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1413}}; ἀ. ἀδικημάτων **unpunished for** offences, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 79, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.76.}}
**3** **unharmed by**, ἀθῷος τῆς Φιλίππου.. δυναστείας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.270.}}
**II** **not deserving punishment, guiltless**, ἀ. ὁ κτείνων{{NT+3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 257; ἀ. χερσί LXX.Psa.23(24).4; ἀ. ἀπὸ τοῦ αἵματος NT.Matt.27.24.}}
**III** _active_, **causing no harm, harmless**, κίνδυνος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 26. (ἀθῷος distinguished by Grammars from Ἄθωος}}, **of Mt. Athos**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 285, cf. Hdn.Gr. 1.128.)}}

124:
** Αἰγύπτιος**, α, ον,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Egyptian”, 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.:}}_adverb_ -ίως **in Egyptian style**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.30.}}
**2** αἰγυπτία, ἡ, name of an ointment, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.643, etc. [In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus Αἰγυπτίη, Αἰγυπτίων}}, etc., are _trisyllable_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.83, etc.]}}

125:
** Αἴγυπτος**, ὁ,
**the river Nile**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.477, +others}}
**2** **King Aegyptus**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 9, etc.}}
**II** ἡ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Egypt”, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.448, etc.}}; Αἴγυπτόνδε **to Egypt**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 426.}}

127:
** αἰδώς**, όος,
_contraction_ οῦς, η (late _nominative_ _plural_ αἰδοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 386)}}, as a moral feeling, **reverence, awe, respect** for the feeling or opinion of others or for one's own conscience, and so **shame, self-respect** (in full ἑαυτοῦ αἰδώς{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 9p.433M.)}}, **sense of honour**, αἰδῶ θέσθ᾽ ἐνὶ θυμῷ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.561}}; ἴσχε γὰρ αἰ. καὶ δέος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 657, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 179, etc.; αἰ. σωφροσύνης πλεῖστον μετέχει, αἰσχύνης δὲ εὐψυχία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 911, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1108a32, etc.; αἰδοῖ μειλιχίῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.172; so ἀλλά με κωλύει αἴδως 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55 (Sapphus est versus); ἅμα κιθῶνι ἐκδυομένῳ συνεκδύεται καὶ τὴν αἰδῶ γυνή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}; δακρύων πένθιμον αἰδῶ tears of grief and **shame**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 579; αἰ. τίς μ᾽ ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217d; αἰ. καὶ δίκη [prev. author] “Prt.” 322c; αἰδοῦς ἐμπίπλασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4}}; **sobriety, moderation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.115; αἰδῶ λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 345.}}
**2** **regard for others, respect, reverence**, αἰδοῦς οὐδεμιῆς ἔτυχον{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1266, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 460}}; αἰ. τοκέων **respect for** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.218}}; τὴν ἐμὴν αἰδῶ **respect** for me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 699}}; **regard** for friends, αἰδοῦς ἀχαλκεύτοισιν ἔζευκται πέδαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 595}}; especially **regard for the helpless, compassion,** αἰδοῦς κῦρσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 247}}; **forgiveness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 867e (compare αἰδέομαι 11.2).}}
**II** **that which causes shame** or **respect**, and so,
**II.1** **shame, scandal**, αἰδώς, Ἀργεῖοι, κάκ᾽ ἐλέγχεα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.787, etc.}}; αἰδώς, ὦ Λύκιοι· πόσε φεύγετε{{Refs|16.422; αἰδὼς μὲν νῦν ἥδε.. 17.336.}}
**II.2** ={τὰ αἰδοῖα}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.262, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 493, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.72.}}
**II.3** **dignity, majesty** αἰ. καὶ χάρις{{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 214.}}
**III** Αἰδώς personified, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Reverence”, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.44; “Mercy”, Ζηνὶ σύνθακος θρόνων Αἰ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1268, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.17.1; παρθένος Αἰδοῦς Δίκη λέγεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 943e.}}

128:
** Αἰθίοψ**, οπος, ὁ,
_feminine_ Αἰθιοπίς, ίδος, ἡ (Αἰθίοψ as _feminine_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 328, 329)}}: _plural_ Αἰθιοπῆες{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.423, whence _nominative_ Αἰθιοπεύς 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 208}}: (αἴθω, ὄψ):—properly, **Burnt-face**, i.e. **Ethiopian, negro**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; proverbial, Αἰθίοπα σμήχειν 'to wash a blackamoor white', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 28.}}
**2** **a fish**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 109.}}
**II** _adjective_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Ethiopian”, Αἰθιοπὶς γλῶσσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.19; γῆ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 300, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 228.4:}}_substantive_ Αἰθιοπίς, ἡ, title of Epic poem in the Homeric cycle; also name of a plant, **silver sage, Salvia argentea**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.104}}:— also Αἰθιόπιος, α, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 349}}: Αἰθιοπικός, ή, όν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, etc.}}; Αἰ. κύμινον, ={ἄμι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.17, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.62}}:—_substantive_ Αἰθιοπία, ἡ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, etc.}}
**II.2** **red-brown,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.196 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), cf. Ach. Tat. 4.5.}}

129:
** αἷμα**, ατος, τό,
**blood**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.303, etc.; φόνος αἵματος 16.162; ψυχῆς ἄκρατον αἷμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 786}}: in _plural_, **streams of blood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1293, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 121, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1172, Alcaeus496.}}
**2** of **anything like blood**, Βακχίου{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Fragmenta” 7; αἷ. σταφυλῆς LXX.Sir.39.26, compare “App.Anth.” 3.166 (5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus).}}
**2.b** **dye** obtained from ἄγχουσα, **alkanet,** {{Refs|PHolm. 15.25, “PLeid.X.” 99.3.}}
**3** with collateral meaning of **spirit, courage**, οὐκ ἔχων αἷμα **pale, spiritless** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.160; τοὺς αἷμα φάσκοντας τὴν ψυχήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 405b4.}}
**II** **bloodshcd, murder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 520, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 101}}; ὅμαιμον αἷ. a kinsman's **murder**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 449}}; εἴργασται μητρῷον αἷ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 285, compare 406}}; αἶ. πράττειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1139; αἷμα συγγενὲς κτείνας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 799.3}}; αἷμα τραγοκτόνον shedding of goat's **blood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 139}}; ἐφ᾽ αἵματι φεύγειν to avoid trial for **murder** by going into exile, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 58 (Milet., 5th c.BC), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105; αἷμα συγγενὲς φεύγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 148}}: _plural_ in this sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 66, 650, frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, never in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; αἵματασύγγονα brothers' **corpses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1502:}} —concrete, νεακόνητον αἷ. keen-edged **death**, i. e. a sword, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1394 (explanation by μάχαιρα, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus).}}
**III** **blood relationship, kin**, αἷ. τε καὶ γένος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.583; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 4.611; οΐ σῆς ἐξ αἵματός εἰσι γενέθλης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.111}}; τὸ αἷ. τινος his **blood** or **origin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.34}}; αἷ. ἐμφύλιον incestuous **kinship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1406; τοὺς πρὸς αἵματος [prev. author] “Aj.” 1305, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1262a11}}; μητρὸς τῆς ἐμῆς ἐν αἵματι **akin** to her **by blood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 606, compare “Th.” 141; ἀφ᾽ αἵματος ὑμετέρου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 245.}}
**III.2** concrete, of a person, ὦ Διὸς.. αἷμα{{Refs|“IG” 14.1003.1, compare 1389 ii4, etc.}}

134:
** αἰνέω**
(compare αἴνημι, αἰνίζομαι), _imperfect_ ᾔνουν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1154}}, _Ionic dialect_ αἴνεον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73}}: _future_ αἰνήσω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.380, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1080, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.72}}; in _Attic dialect_ Poets always αἰνέσω, as in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.63, 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.112}}: _aorist_ ᾔνησα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _optative_ αἰνήσειε 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 57.1}}; _Doric dialect_ αἴνησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.13}}; in _Attic dialect_ always ᾔνεσα, _Ionic dialect_ αἴνεσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.113:}}_perfect_ ᾔνεκα (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.207:}} — _middle_, _future_ αἰνέσομαι (only in compounds ἐπ-, παρ-):—_passive_, _aorist_ _participle_ αἰνεθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.102:}}_perfect_ ᾔνημαι (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.233.}}—poetical and _Ionic dialect_ Verb, very rare in good _Attic dialect_ Prose {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404d, “Lg.” 952c), ἐπαινέω}} being used instead:—properly, **tell, speak of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 98, 1482 (both Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 192}}; σε κρηγύην αἰνεῖ **reports of** you as honest, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.47.}}
**II** usually **praise, approve**, opposed to νεικέω, ψέγω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.249, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 612, etc.; ἀνδρὸς ὃν οὐδ᾽ αἰνεῖν τοῖσι κακοῖσι θέμις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 673}}:— _passive_, ὑπὸ Σιμωνίδεω αἰνεθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.102; ἐπ᾽ ἔργμασιν ἐσθλοῖς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.15.}}
**II.b** especially in religious sense, **glorify** God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ch.16.4, NT.Luke.2.13, “PMag.Par.” 1.1146, +others:—also with _dative_, τῷ κυρίῳ, τῷ θεῷ, LXX.1Ch.16.36, NT.Rev.19.5.}}
**II.2** **approve, advise, recommend** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.380, 403}}: with _infinitive_, **recommend** to do a thing, euphemistic for κελεύω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 555, 715: with}}_participle_, αἰνεῖν ἰόντα **to commend** one's going, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 643:}} —ὦ δεινὸν αἶνον αἰνέσας **giver of** dire **counsel**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1380}}:— with _accusative_ _of things_, **to be content with, acquiesce in**, γάμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.13, compare “N.” 1.72, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 469, “Supp.” 902, 1070 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1157; θῆσσαν τράπεζαν αἰνέσαι [prev. author] Alcaeus2.}}
**II.3** **praise**, with collateral sense, **decline courteously**, νῇ ὀλίγην αἰνεῖν, μεγάλῃ δ᾽ ἐνὶ φορτία θέσθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 643 (compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.22f), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 109}}; but, **thank**, conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 388.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **approve**, ὁ δᾶμος αἰνεῖ{{Refs|“IG” 9(1).119 (_Locrian dialect_).}}
**III** **to promise** or **vow**, τινί τι or τινὶ ποιεῖν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1398, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 12.}}

135:
** αἴνιγμα**, ατος, τό,
**dark saying, riddle**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 177, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 610, etc., cf. LXX.Deu.28.37: frequently in _plural_, ἐξ αἰνιγμάτων}} **in riddles, darkly**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1112, 1183; δι᾽ αἰνιγμάτων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.121 (variant), etc.; ἐν αἰνίγματι NT.1Cor.13.12}}; αἴ. προβάλλειν, ξυντιθέναι, πλέκειν **to make** a riddle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 162b, “Ap.” 27a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.988a}}; opposed to διειπεῖν, εἰδέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 393, 1525; μαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 48.}}
**II** **taunt**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.27.}}
**III** **ambush** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|(from Theban), 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 4.}}

136:
** αἶνος**, ὁ,
(αἰνέω) _poetry_ and _Ionic dialect_ word, **tale, story**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.652, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.508, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 534 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἰνεῖν αἶνον to tell **a tale**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1483 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1380}}: especially **story with moral, fable**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 202, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86, 89; ἄκουε δὴ τὸν αἶνον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.211}}: generally, **saying, proverb**, παλαιὸς αἶ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 508, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.43}}; **riddle,** {{Refs|Carm.Pop. 34.}}
**II** = _Attic dialect_ ἔπαινος, **praise**, [{{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.795, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.110, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.6; ἐπιτύμβιος αἶ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1547, compare 780, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 707 (all Lyric poetry); ἄξιος αἴνου μεγάλου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.107 (variant{ἐπαίνου}),cf. LXX.Psa.8.2, +others, NT.Luke.18.43.}}]
**III** **decree, resolution**, τῶν Ἀχαιῶν{{Refs|“IG” 4.926 (Epid.)}}; κατ᾽ αἶνον, opposed to κατὰ ψήφισμα, {{Refs|“SIG” 672.15 (Delph.), compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 36.16.}}

138:
** αἱρέω**,
_imperfect_ ᾕρεον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.579}}, _Ionic dialect_ αἵρεον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.31, but _contraction_ ᾕρει even in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.463, ᾕρευν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 302}}: _future_ αἱρήσω{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.28, etc.:}}_aorist 1_ ᾕρησα late (ἀν-) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.40, etc.}}: _perfect_ ᾕρηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 267, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.61, etc., _Ionic dialect_ ἀραίρηκα or αἵρηκα}} (ἀν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.102}}: _pluperfect_ ἀραιρήκεε{{Refs|3.39}}:—_middle_, _future_ αἱρήσομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.235, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ᾑρησάμην{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.13.7 assuming variant, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.53, etc.}}: _perfect_ in _middle_ sense ᾕρημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1577, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15, etc.}}: _3rd.pers.plural_ _pluperfect_ ᾕρηντο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62}}:—_passive_, _future_ αἱρεθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 234b; rarely ᾑρήσομαι [prev. author] “Prt.” 338c:}}_aorist_ ᾑρέθην and _perfect_ ᾕρημαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.146, +others}}; _perfect_ _participle_ ἀραιρημένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.66: plqf. ᾕρηντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.1, ἀραίρητο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.191, etc.}} —From [root]ἑλ-: _future_ ἑλῶ only late (δι-) {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 6.18}}, (ἀν-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.18}}, (καθ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 4.334 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus):}}_aorist 1_ εἷλα (ἀν-) {{NT|NT.Act.2.23}}, (ἀν-) {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 314.24 (from Smyrna)}}: elsewhere_aorist 2_ εἷλον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.561, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ ἕλον{{Refs|17.321}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἕλεσκε{{Refs|24.752}}:— _middle_, _future_ ἑλοῦμαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.75}}, (ἀφ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Timostratus Comicus 5}}, (δι-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.60}}, (ἐξ-) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.9}}: _aorist 1_ εἱλάμην{{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 314.5 (from Smyrna)}}, (ἀφ-) variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.546a}}, (δι-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.56 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}: elsewhere _aorist 2_ εἱλόμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.139, etc.}}, _2nd pers.singular_ ἤλεο[{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787.6.3:—Cretan dialect forms αἰλεθῇ “Leg.Gort.” 2.21, ἀν-αιλῆθαι [prev. work]7.10, +others:}} —the _etymology_ is doubtful, and ἀγρέω (which see) probably has a different root.]
**A** _active_, **take with the hand, grasp, seize**, αἱ. τι ἐν χερσίν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.66}}; αἱ. τινὰ χειρός **to take** one by the hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.323}}; κόμης τινά{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 197; μ᾽ ἑλὼν ἐπὶ μάστακα χερσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.76:}}_participle_ ἑλών adverbially, κατακτεῖναί μ᾽ ἑλών{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 497; ἄξω ἑλών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.139, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.1}}; but ἔνθεν ἑλών **having taken up** [the song], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.500.}}
**A.2** **take away**, ἀπ᾽ ἀπήνης ᾕρεον ἄποινα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.579.}}
**A.II** **take, get into one's power**, νῆας {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.42}}; especially **take** a city,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 347, etc.}}; **overpower, kill**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.457, etc.; ἕλοιμί κεν ἤ κε ἁλοίην 22.253}}:—frequently of passions, etc., **come upon, seize**, χόλος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.322; ἵμερος 3.446; ὕπνος 10.193; λήθη 2.34, etc.: with double _accusative_, τὸν δ᾽ ἄτη φρένας εἷλε 16.805}}; of disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142b.}}
**A.II.2** **catch, take**, ζωὸν ἑλεῖν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.102}}; **take in hunting**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 302, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, etc.}}; **overtake**, in a race, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.345}}; **get into one's power, entrap**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 764, etc.}}; in good sense, **win over**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.16, compare 3.11.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 205e, etc.}}
**A.II.2.b** with _participle_, **catch, detect** one doing a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 385, 655; ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ ἑλεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1214; φῶρα ἐπὶ κλοπῇ ἑλεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 874b.}}
**A.II.3** generally, **win, gain**, κῦδος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.321; στεφάνους 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.74, etc.; especially in games, Ἴσθμι᾽ ἑλὼν πύξ 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 158}}; with double sense, **overcome** and **win**, ἑλέτην δίφρον τε καὶ ἀνέρε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.328; ἕλεν Οἰνομάου βίαν παρθένον τε σύνευνον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.88, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 353:}} — _passive_, ἁγὼν ᾑρέθη the fight **was won**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1148.}}
**A.II.3.b** generally, **get, obtain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359a, “Ti.” 64b, etc.}}
**A.II.4** as law-term, **convict**, τινά τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 591, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.36, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.156; εἷλέ σ᾽ ἡ Δίκη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heracl.” 941, compare “Supp.” 608: with}}_participle_, αἱ. τινὰ κλέπτοντα **to convict** of theft, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 829, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 941d}}; ᾑρῆσθαι κλοπεύς (i.e. ὤν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 493, compare 406.}}
**A.II.4.b** αἱ. δίκην, γραφήν **get a verdict for conviction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5, etc.}}; also ἑλεῖν τινα **obtain a conviction against** one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.13}}; ἑλεῖν τὰ διαμαρτυρηθ έντα **convict** the evidence of falsehood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.15.}}
**A.II.4.c** _absolutely_, **get a conviction**, οἱ ἑλόντες, opposed to οἱ ἑαλωκότες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.11}}; δολίοις ἕλε Κύπρις λόγοις Aphrodite **won her cause**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 289, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762b, etc.}}
**A.II.4.d** of a thing or circumstances which **convict**, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν ὃ ἐμὲ αἱρεῖ{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 28a.}}
**A.II.5** ὁ λόγος αἱρέει reason or the reason of the thing **proves**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33}}: with _accusative_ _person_, reason **persuades** one, i.e. it **seems good** to one, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.132, 7.41}}; ὡς ἐμὴ γνώμη αἱ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.43; ὅπῃ ὁ λόγος αἱ. βέλτιστ᾽ ἂν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604c, compare “Lg.” 663d}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“R.” 440b; ὁ αἱρῶν λόγος 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.92}}; αἱρεῖ alone, **proves**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.651b.}}
**A.II.5.b** τὸ αἱροῦν the sum **due,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 167.25 (1st c.AD)}}; τὰ αἱροῦντα [τάλαντα]{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 2.23.14 (2nd c.BC), “PRyl.” 88.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**A.III** **grasp with the mind, understand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 17e, 20d, “Plt.” 282d.}}
**B** _middle_, with _perfect_ ᾕρημαι (see. above), **take for oneself**, ἔγχος ἑλέσθαι **take one's** spear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.140, etc.; ἐκ γαίας λίθον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199}}; δόρπον, δεῖπνον **take one's** supper, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.370, 2.399}}; πιέειν δ᾽ οὐκ εἶχεν ἑλέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.584}}; Τρωσὶν.. ὅρκον ἑλ. **obtain** it from.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.119}}; and so in most senses of the _active_, with the reflexive force added.
**B.II** **take to oneself, choose**, ἕταρον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.235, compare 9.139, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.149, etc.}}; **prefer**, τι πρό τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; τι ἀντί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15; τί τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1101, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.49.}}
**B.II.b** with _infinitive_, **prefer** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, etc.; ἑλέσθαι μᾶλλον τεθνάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38e; μᾶλλον ἂν ἕλοιτό μ᾽ ἢ τοὺς πάντας Ἀργείους λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 47: without μᾶλλον, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.59, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.62.}}
**B.II.c** αἱ. εἰ.. **to be content** if., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.68 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.II.2** αἱ. τά τινων **take** another's part, **join** their **party**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63, etc.}}; αἱ. γνώμην **to adopt** an opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.137.}}
**B.II.3** **choose by vote, elect** to an office, αἱ. τινὰ δικαστήν, στρατηγόν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.96, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 117, etc.}}; τινὰς ἀριστίνδην Legal cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.57; αἱ. τινὰ ἐπ᾽ ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 90b; αἱ. τινὰ ἄρχειν [prev. author] “Ap.” 28e, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.127.}}
**C** _passive_, **to be taken**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185, 191, 9.102}}; more commonly ἁλίσκομαι.
**C.2** see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3.}}
**C.II** _passive_ to _middle_ sense, **to be chosen**, in _perfect_ ᾕρημαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1209, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἀραίρημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.118, 172, 173, +others; στρατηγεῖν ᾑρημένος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.1}}; ἐπ᾽ ἀρχῆς ᾑρῆσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.3.2; ἐπὶ τὴν τῶν παίδων ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 809a}}; τοῦ ἔτους.. ᾑρημένοι **elected** for the year.., {{Refs|“IGRom.” 3.1422 (Bithyn.):}} —_aorist_ ᾑρέθην is always so used, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 505, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 799, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31, etc.}}; _present_ rarely, αἱροῦνται πρεσβευταί **are chosen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299a19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.16.}}

139:
** αἵρεσις**, εως, ἡ,
**taking**, especially of a town, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.1, etc.}}; ἡ βασιλέος αἵ. **the taking by** the king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.3; ἐλπίζων ταχίστην -σιν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75}}; αἵ. δυνάμεως **acquisition** of power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 513a}}:—generally, **taking, receiving**, ἐπιγενημάτων{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.66 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B** (αἱρέομαι) **choice**, αἵρεσίν τ᾽ ἐμοὶ δίδου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 779; τῶνδε.. αἵρεσιν παρδίδωμι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.82}}; followed by _relative_, αἵ. διδόναι ὁκοτέρην.., εἰ.., etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.19}}; αἵ. προτιθέναι, προβάλλειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 196c, “Sph.” 245b; εἰ νέμοι τις αἵρεσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 265; αἵρεσιν λαβεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.11; ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.19; αἵ. γίγνεταί τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61}}; οὐκ ἔχει αἵρεσιν it admits no **choice**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.708b.}}
**B.2** **choice, election** of magistrates, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a26, +others; αἱρέσει, opposed to κλήρῳ, 1300a19, etc.}}
**B.3** **inclination, choice**, πρός τινα Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.166, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.61.9, etc., compare “IG” 2.591b}}; opposed to φυγή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.62U.}}; περὶ αἱρέσεων καὶ φυγῶν, title of treatise by Epicurus.
**B.II** **purpose, course of action** or **thought**, like{προαίρεσις}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256c; ἡ αἵ. τῆς πρεσβείας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.11}}; αἵ. Ἐλληνική **the study** of Greek literature, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.1.3}}:—**conduct,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 28.10 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.II.2** **system of philosophic principles**, or **those who profess such principles, sect, school**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.93.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29, Polystr. p.20 W., 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 1.7, “Comp.” 2, +others, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 15.16.3}}; κατὰ τῶν αἱ., title of treatise by Antipater of Tarsus; περὶ αἱρέσεων, title of Menippean satire by Varro,{{Refs|compare “fragment” 164}}; αἵρεσις πρὸς Γοργιππίδην, title of work by Chrysippus, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.191}}; especially **religious party** or **sect**, of the Essenes, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.1; the Sadducees and Pharisees, NT.Act.5.17, 15.5, 26.5; the Christians, [prev. work] 24.5, 14, 28.22}}, generally, **faction, party**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.2.}}
**B.II.3** **corps** of epheboi, {{Refs|“OGI” 176 (from Egypt).}}
**B.II.4** Astrology texts, **'condition'**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 21; ἡ ἡμερινὴ αἵ. Vett. Val. 1.13.}}
**B.III** **proposed condition, proposal**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.10.}}
**B.III.2** **commission**, ἡ ἐπὶ τοὺς νέους αἵ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367a}}; **embassy, mission,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.937 (Epid.).}}
**B.III.3** **freewill offering**, opposed to **vow**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.22.18, +others}}
**B.III.4** **bid** at auction, τὴν ἀμείνονα αἵ. διδόντι παραδοθῆναι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 716.22 (2nd c.AD), compare 1630.8 (3rd c.AD).}}

141:
** αἱρετ-ικός**, ή, όν,
(αἱρέω) **able to choose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 412a}}; **due to choice**, οἰκείωσις{{Refs|Hierocl. p.41.5A., Anonymus “in Tht.” 7.40.}}
**2** **factious,** {{NT|NT.Tit.3.10.}}
**3** Astrology texts, **belonging to the 'condition'**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “Q.” 2.}}
**4** _adverb_ -κῶς **from choice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.126, 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor p.41.7 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}

142:
** ἀείρω**,
_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and _poetry_; αἴρω (once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, see below)}}, _Attic dialect_ and Trag. (except {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 759, “Pers.” 660, both Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀέρρω, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 78:}}_imperfect_ ἤειρον (συν-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.499, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125}}, _Epic dialect_ ἄειρον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.386}}, _Attic dialect_ and Trag. ᾖρον: _future_ ἀρῶ [ᾱ], _contraction_ for ἀερῶ (which is not found), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 795, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 322, “Tr.” 1148, probably in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 14}}: _aorist 1_ ἤειρα (συν-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.590}}, (παρ-) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 94, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 9.13}}, _Epic dialect_ ἄειρα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.730}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _imperative_ ἀέρρατε{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 91}}; _subjunctive_ ἀέρσῃ{{Refs|Panyasis Epicus 13.13}}; _participle_ ἀείρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 418; also ἄηρα “IG” 12(3).449 (from Thera)}}; ἦρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 47, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, etc.}}, _3rd.pers.plural_ ἤροσαν{{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Josh.3.14, _optative_ ἄραις 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.71}}, _infinitive_ ἆραι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 35}}, _participle_ ἄρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12, etc., Cretan dialect ἤραντας “GDI” 5015 (Gort.) [ᾱ-}} in all moods]: _perfect_ ἦρκα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.52}}, (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.100}}_pluperfect_ ἤρκεσαν (ἀπ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.150}}:—_middle_ ἀείρομαι (ἀπ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.563, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 216 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἴρομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 360, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60: fut ἀροῦμαι [ᾱ] 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1597}}: _aorist 1_ _imperative_ ἀείραο{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.746}}_infinitive_ ἀείρασθαι (ἀντ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.212}}, _participle_ **-άμενος**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.856, “IG” 4.952.112 (Epid.)}}; also ἠράμην [ᾱ- in all moods] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.510, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.107, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 986, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 525, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 374e, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ ἄρατο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.5}}: _perfect_ ἦρμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 54}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 450 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.174}}: _future_ ἀρθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 565}}: _aorist_ ἠέρθην{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1651}}, (παρ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.341}}, _Epic dialect_ ἀέρθην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.540}}, _3rd.pers.plural_ ἄερθεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.74}}, _subjunctive_ ἀερθῶ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 848 (Lyric poetry)}}, _participle_ ἀερθείς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.375, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.75, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1525 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.1, etc.; also ἤρθην 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 111, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 214 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.42, etc.}}, _participle_ ἀρθείς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.63}}, (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἤερμαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.171: _Epic dialect_}}_pluperfect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ ἄωρτο (for ἤορτο) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.272, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.43, ἔωρτο 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus [ἀείρω}} has ᾰ, except in late poetry, as [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.347.] (ἀείρω ={ἀ-ϝερ-} yω,compare αὐειρομέναι 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 23.63}}]; αἴρω (oncein [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.724 in _participle_ αἴροντας) may ={ϝαρ-yω} for ϝγ[νυλλ]-ψω from the reduced form of the root, but is more probably an analogical formation arising from the contracted forms. Fut. ἀροῦμαι [ᾰ] and}}]_aorist_ ἀρόμην, ἤρετο, etc., _infinitive_ ἀρέσθαι [ᾰ], belong to ἄρνυμαι, which see; ἤρᾰτο may have displaced ἤρετο in Hom,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis ad 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.373}}. The sense **attach** found in compounds συν-, παρ-αείρω is probably derived from the use see.{{Refs|1.)}}
**I** _active_, **lift, raise up**, νέκυν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.724}}; ὑψόσ᾽ ἀείρας [κυνέην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|] 10.465; πίνακας παρέθηκεν ἀείρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.141; Εὐμάστας με ἄηρεν ἀπὸ χθονός “IG” 12(3).449, inscription on a stone (from Thera)}}; ἀπὸ γῆς αἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 90a}}; ἱστία στεῖλαν ἀείραντες furled by **brailing them up**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.11}}; but ἀ. ἱστία **hoist** sail, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1229; αἴ. κεραίας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.12; εὔμαριν ἀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 660}}; κοῦφον αἴ. βῆμα **walk lightly, trip**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 342}}; αἴ. σκέλη, of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.15, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 710b20; ὀρθὸν αἴ. τὸ κάρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 496; ὀφθαλμὸν ἄρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 795}}; ἄρασα μύξας, of a deer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 89; ὀφρῦς αἴροντα 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 85}}; αἴ. σημεῖον **make** a signal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.23}}; αἴ. μηχανήν, in the theatre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 191.15; so ἐπὶ τὰς μηχανὰς καταφεύγουσι θεοὺς αἴροντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 425d}}; τεῖχος ἱκανὸν αἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, compare 2.75:}} —frequently in _participle_, ἄρας ἔπαισε he **raised** [them] and struck, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1270; ἡ βουλὴ ἄρασα τὴν ἀφ᾽ ἱερᾶς ἀφῆκεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 32, cf. NT.1Cor.6.15}}:—_passive_, ἐς αἰθέρα δῖαν ἀέρθη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.540, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.74; ὑψόσ᾽ ἀερθείς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.432; ἔμπνους ἀρθείς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.9; φρυκτοὶ ᾔροντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94, cf. Aen. Tact. 26.14}}; **mount up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5}}; ἄνω ἀρθῆναι, of the sun, **to be high** in heaven, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6}} **to be seized, snatched up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 565.}}
**I.2** **take up**, in various uses: **draw** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1339}}; **gather** food, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 707}}; **pluck**herbs, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.287, +others}}
**I.3** **take up and carry** or **bring**, ἐκ βελέων Σαρπηδόνα δῖον ἀείρας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.678; νόσφιν ἀειράσας 24.583}}; ἄχθος ἀ. **convey**, of ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.312}}; μῆλα ἐξ᾽ Ιθάκης ἄειραν νηυσί **carried** them **off**, {{Refs|21.18}}; μή μοι οἶνον ἄειρε **bring** me not wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.264.}}
**I.4** **take up and bear**, as a burden, μόρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 547; ἆθλον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 80; ἄλγος 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.65.}}
**I.4.b** **wear** clothes, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.2.28, +others}}
**I.5** of armies or fleets, τὰς ναῦς αἴ. **get** the ships **under sail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.52}}; especially _intransitive_, **get under way, set out**, ἆραι τῷ στρατῷ{{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 23}}:—_passive_, ἀερθῆναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.52; ἀερθέντες ἐκ.. 1.165; ἀ. εἰς.. 1.170; ἐφ᾽ ἡμετέρᾳ γᾷ ἀρθείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 111 (Lyric poetry)}}; but ἀερθείς **carried too far**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.75.}}
**I.6** **raise, levy**, λεκτὸν ἀροῦμεν στόλον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 795.}}
**I.7** **rear** a child, τοῖς τοκεῦσί σ᾽ ἤειρα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 9.13.}}
**II** **raise up, exalt**, ἀπὸ σμικροῦ δ᾽ ἂν ἄρειας μέγαν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 262, compare 791}}; ὄλβον ὅν Δαρεῖος ἦρεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 164}}:—especially of pride and passion, **exalt, excite**, ὑψοῦ αἴ. θυμόν **grow excited**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 914}}; αἴ. θάρσος **pluck up** courage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1598}}:—_passive_, **to be raised, increased**, ἡ δύναμις ᾔρετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118; ᾔρετο τὸ ὕψος τοῦ τείχους μέγα [prev. author] 2.75}}; ἤρθη μέγας **rose** to greatness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.8; οὐκ ἤρθη νοῦν ἐς ἀτασθαλίην 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 111}}; ἀρθῆναι φόβῳ, δείμασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 214, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 69: _absolutely_, ἀείρομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 216 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1180.}}
**II.2** **raise by words**, hence, **praise, extol**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 322, etc.}}; αἴ. λόγῳ **to exaggerate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.71.}}
**III** **lift and take away, remove**, ἀπό με τιμᾶν ἦραν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 847; τινὰ ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 578e}}; generally, **take away, put an end to**, κακά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 942}}; τραπέζας αἴ. **clear away** dinner, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 273; ἀρθέντος τοῦ αἰτίου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 920b11}}; **deny** (opposed to τίθημι **posit**), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.10}}; Delph. and _Locrian dialect_ _perfect_ _passive_ _participle_ ἀρμένος **cancelled, null and void**, ὠνὰ ἀ. καὶ ἄκυρος{{Refs|“GDI” 1746 (Delph.); ἀτελὴς καὶ ἀ. “IG” 9(1).374 (from Naupactus).}}
**III.2** **make away with, destroy,** {{NT|NT.Matt.24.39; ἆρον, ἆρον}}**away with him!** {{NT|NT.John.19.15}}; ἐκ τῶν ζώντων αἴ. {{Refs|“Tab.Defix.Aud.” 1.18.}}
**IV** _middle_, **lift, take up for oneself** or **what is one's own**, [πέπλων] ἕν᾽ ἀειραμένη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.293}}; hence, **carry off, win**, πάντας ἀειράμενος πελέκεας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.856; ἄρατο νίκαν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2.5; ἠρμένοι νίκην 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.13.}}
**IV.2** ὄγκον ἄρασθαι **to be puffed up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 129; θαυμαστὸν ὄγκον ἀράμενοι τοῦ μύθου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277b.}}
**IV.3** **raise, lift**, τύπωμα ἠρμένοι χεροῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 54}}; κανοῦν αἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 850; βοῦς “IG” 22.1028.28, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 27.5}}; ῥόθιον **raise** a surging cheer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 546; Σαμόσατα ἀράμενος μετέθηκεν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 24}}; ἀείρεσθαι τὰ ἱστία **hoist** sail, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.56, compare 94.}}
**IV.4** **raise, stir up**, νεῖκος ἀειράμενος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 90, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 986, 991}}; **begin, undertake**, πόλεμον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp..” 342, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.132, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.5}} (_passive_, πόλεμος αἴρεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1188); κίνδυνον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.63}}; φυγὴν αἴρεσθαι **take to** flight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 481, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 54.}}
**IV.5** **take upon oneself, undergo**, πόνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 907; πένθος [prev. author] “OT” 1225; βάρος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 473.}}
**IV.6** _absolutely_, βαρὺς ἀ. slow to **undertake anything**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150.}}
**IV.7** **take away, remove**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1201}}; hence, **kill** or **destroy**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.4, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.3; πόλιν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.23.}}
**V** _passive_, **to be suspended, hang**, [μάχαιρα] πὰρ ξίφεος μέγα κουλεὸν αἰὲν ἄωρτο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.272, 19.253.}}
**V.2** in Medicine texts, **to be swollen**, [σπλὴν] ἀερθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.61; μαζοὶ ἀείρονται [prev. work] 2.174.}}

143:
** αἰσθ-άνομαι**
(compare αἴσθομαι), _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers.plural_ _optative_ αἰσθανοίατο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 209}}: _imperfect_ ῃσθανόμην: _future_ αἰσθήσομαι{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 75, etc.; later αἰσθανθήσομαι LXX.Isa.49.26; αἰσθηθήσομαι [prev. work] 33.11:}}_aorist 2_ ᾐσθόμην: _perfect_ ᾔσθημαι: later, _aorist 1_ ᾐσθησάμην{{LXX+4th c.BC+|Scholia 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 418; ᾐσθήθην LXX.Job.40.18: (compare ἀΐω)}}:—**perceive, apprehend by the senses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Alcmaeon Philosophus 1a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.87, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 11, etc.; τῇ ὄψει, τῇ ἁφῇ, τῇ ἀκοῇ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 1}}; αἰ. τῇ ἀκοῇ, τῇ ὀσμῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.8; see, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 75, etc.}}; **hear**, βοήν{{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1318, compare “Ph.” 252; οὐκ εἶδον αὐτόν, ᾐσθόμην δ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ὄντανιν [prev. work] 445}}; τινὸς ὑποστενούσης αἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 79; βοῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 603, etc.}}
**2** of mental perception, **perceive, understand**, τῇ γνώμῃ αἰσθέσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 1; τὸ πραχθέν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.4, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36, etc.}}:— **hear, learn**, see below {{Refs|11: _absolutely_, αἰσθάνει}}**you are right**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 752}}; ᾔσθημαι, in parenthesis, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1403.}}
**II** Construct. in both senses, with _genitive_, **take notice of, have perception of**, τῶν κακῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 638 assuming variant; rarely περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70}}; αἰ. ὑπό τινος **learn** from one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.2; διά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184e, +others: with _accusative_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 89, “Ph.” 252, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 653, 764, etc.}}:—frequently with _participle_ agreeing with subject, αἰσθάνομαι κάμνων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51; αἰσθώμεθα γελοῖοι ὄντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 122c}}; agreeing with object, τυράννους ἐκπεσόντας ᾐσθόμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 957, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.47, etc.; ἤδη τινῶν ᾐσθόμην ἀχθομένων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22c; ᾐσθόμην τεχνωμένου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 176}}: less frequently with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.59}}; αἰ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21e, etc.}}; ᾔσθετο ὅτι τὸ στράτευμα ἦν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.21; αἰ. ὡς.. [prev. work] 3.1.40, etc.}}; οὕνεκα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1477:}} — _absolutely_, αἰσθανόμενος **having full possession of one's faculties**, τῇ ἡλικίᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; **sensible, of keen perception**, καὶ μετρίως αἰσθανομένῳ φανερόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.1.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 360d.}} —The _passive_ is supplied by αἴσθησιν παρέχω,compare αἴσθησις{{Refs|1.}}
**III** **display feeling**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b37.}}

144:
** αἴσθ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**sense-perception, sensation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 13, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 99b35}}; τοῦ σώματος ἡ αἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9}}; πρὸς αἴσθησιν **perceptibly**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 1.10, etc.}}: in _plural_, **the senses**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9, +others}}; δι᾽ ἑπτὰ σχημάτων αἱ αἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 156b, etc.: in _sing._ of the several senses, ἡ τοῦ ὁρᾶν αἴ. [prev. author] “R.” 507e}}; ἀπ᾽ ὄψεως ἤ τινος ἄλλῃς αἰ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 39b}}; **organ** or **seat of sensation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.6; πάσα, τὰς αἰ. ἐν τῇ κεφαλῇ εἶναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 95, compare “Pr.” 958b16}}; αἴ. πημάτων **perception, sense of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 290}}; especially of **pain**, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 113.10. +others}}; also of the mind, **perception, knowledge** of a thing, ἐν αἰ. γενέσθαι τινός{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 11, etc.}}; αἴ. ἔχειν τινός, ={αἰσθάνεσθαί τινος}, have **a perception of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40c; περὶ ὑμῶν “Tht.” 192b; πᾶσαν αἴσθησιν αἰσθάνεσθαι “Phdr.” 240d; λαμβάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.47; ἐν αἰ. εἶναι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.15:}} —also of things, αἴσθησιν ἔχειν give **a perception**, i.e. become **perceptible**, serving as _passive_ to αἰσθάνομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61; more frequently αἴσθησιν παρέχειν [prev. author] 3.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.13, etc.; αἴ. ποιῆσαί τινι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.44, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.7}}; αἴ. παρέχειν τινός furnish **the means of observing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.50; αἴ. ἐγένετο περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.16.}}
**II** in object. sense, **impressions of sense**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 980a22}}; **stage-effects, Po.** {{Refs|1454b16}}; αἰσθήσεις θεῶν **visible appearances** of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111b.}}
**II.2** **display of feeling**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1386a32 (variant).}}
**II.3** in hunting, **scent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.5 (_plural_).}}—Confined to Prose in early writers, except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place], 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 196.5.}}

148:
** αἰσχρο-λογία**, ἡ,
**foul language, obscenity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a23.}}
**2** **abuse**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.11.8, compare “POxy.” 410.77, NT.Col.3.8, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.176S., etc.}}

149:
** αἰσχρός**, ά, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs|“APl.” 4.151}}: (αἶσχος):—in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **causing shame, dishonouring, reproachful**, νείκεσσεν.. αἰσχροῖς ἐπέεσσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.38, etc.}} _adverb_ αἰσχρῶς, ἐνένισπεν{{Refs|23.473.}}
**II** opposed to καλός:
**II.1** of outward appearance, **ugly, ill-favoured**, of Thersites, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.216, compare “hymnus ad Apollinem” 197, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196 (_comparative_)}}, etc.; **deformed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14 (_superlative_)}}; αἰσχρῶς χωλός **with an ugly** lameness, {{Refs|[prev. work] 63: but commonly}}
**II.2** in moral sense, **shameful, base**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 685, etc.; αἰσχροῖς γὰρ αἰσχρὰ πράγματ᾽ ἐκδιδάσκεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 621}}; αἰσχρόν [ἐστι], with _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.298, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 473, etc.}}; αἰσχρόν, εἰ πύθοιτό τις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1159; ἐν αἰσχρῷ θέσθαι τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 806}}; ἐπ᾽ αἰσχροῖς on the ground of **base actions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 188, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 511:}} —τὸ αἰ. as _substantive_, **dishonour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 476}}; τὸ ἐμὸν αἰ. my **disgrace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.9}}; τὸ καλὸν καὶ τὸ αἰ. virtue and **vice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1366a24, etc.}} _adverb_, **shamefully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 989, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183d, etc.}}: _superlative_ αἴσχιστα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 959, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 367.}}
**II.3** **ill-suited**, αἰ. ὁ καιρός{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.178}}; αἰ. πρός τι **awkward** at it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.7; αἰσχρὸν καὶ ἄτεχνον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 30.}}
**III** Regul. _comparative_ and _superlative_ -ότερος, -ότατος are late, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.58S. (probably), 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.587b: elsewhere αἰσχίων, αἴσχιστος}} (formed from a Root αἰσχο-), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.437, 2.216}}; double _superlative_ αἰσχιστότατος{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem commentarii” p.124}} C. _adverb_, _superlative_ αἰσχίστως{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Mnasalcas Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.163a, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.21.}}

150:
** αἰσχρός**, ά, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs|“APl.” 4.151}}: (αἶσχος):—in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **causing shame, dishonouring, reproachful**, νείκεσσεν.. αἰσχροῖς ἐπέεσσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.38, etc.}} _adverb_ αἰσχρῶς, ἐνένισπεν{{Refs|23.473.}}
**II** opposed to καλός:
