151:
** αἰσχρότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**ugliness, deformity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525a.}}
**II** **filthy conduct,** {{NT|NT.Eph.5.4}}; euphemistic for **fellatio**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1308}}:— αἰσχροσύνη, ἡ, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 11.229.}}

152:
** αἰσχύν-η** [ῡ], ἡ,
**shame, dishonour**, ἐς αἰσχύνην φέρει{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, compare 3.133}}; αἰσχύνην φέρει, ἔχει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 66, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 244, etc.}}; αἰ. περιίσταταί με, συμβαίνει μοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.8, 18.85; αἰσχύνῃ πίπτειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 597; περιπίπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.9; αἰσχύνην περιάπτειν τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35a; αἰ. προσβάλλειν τινί [prev. author] “Lg.” 878c; ἐν αἰ. ποιεῖν τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.136; ἡ τῶν πραγμάτων αἰ. 1.27.}}
**2** αἰ. γυναικῶν **dishonouring** of women, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.114 (_plural_), 12.259 (_plural_)}}; γράφεσθαί τινα γένους αἰσχύνης for **dishonour done** to his race, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 919e.}}
**3** concrete, of a person, αἰ. φίλοις, πάτρᾳ, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1272, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 774; ἄνθρωπος αἰ. τῆς πόλεως γεγονώς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.241}}; of a decree, {{Refs|[prev. work]105.}}
**II** **shame for** an ill deed, personified in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 409; Αἰσχύνην οὐ νομίσασα θεόν “Anthologia Graeca” 7.450 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** like{αἰδώς}, **sense of shame, honour**, πᾶσαν αἰ. ἀφείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 120; ἡ γὰρ αἰ. [πάρος] τοῦ ζῆν.. νομίζεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 200}}; δι᾽ αἰσχύνης ἔχειν τι to be **ashamed of**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 683}}; αἰσχύνην ἔχειν τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 616; αἰ. ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 178d; ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.10}}; joined with δέος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1079}}; with ἔλεος and αἰδώς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.27:—rare in _plural_, πτήσσουσαν αἰσχύνῃσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659.9}}; ἐν αἰσχύναις ἔχω I hold it **a shameful thing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 164.}}
**III** later ={αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 365}}; compare τὴν τοῦ σώματος αἰ. Alcid.ap {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406a29.}}

153:
** αἰσχύνω** [ῡ]:
_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ αἰσχύνεσκε (κατ-) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.531}}: _future_ **-ῠνῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 719}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-υνέω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.53}}: _aorist_ ᾔσχῡνα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.571, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4, etc.}}: _perfect_ σχυγκα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.16}}:—_passive_, _future_ αἰσχῠνοῦμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 856, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 200, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 49d, etc., rarely αἰσχυνθήσομαι (see. entry near the end)}}: _aorist_ ᾐσχύνθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} and _Attic dialect_, _poetry_ _infinitive_ αἰσχυνθῆμεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.27:}}_perfect_ ᾔσχυμμαι (see. below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus I)}}:—**make ugly, disfigure**, πρόσωπον, κόμην, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.24, 27, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 529}}; αἰ. τὸν ἵππον **give** the horse **a bad form**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.12.}}
**2** mostly in moral sense, **dishonour, tarnish**, μηδὲ γένος πατέρων αἰσχυνέμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.209, compare 23.571; τὴν Σπάρτην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.53; ξενίαν τράπεζαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 401; τοὺς πρὸς αἵματος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1305; τοὺς πατέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 246d.}}
**2.b** especially **dishonour** a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 44, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 19, etc.; εὐνήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1626}}; εἰς τὸ σῶμα αἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311b7; _absolutely_, “Foed. Delph.Pell.” 2 A 12.}}
**3** **disdain**, ἐπιχώρια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.22.}}
**B** _passive_, **to be dishonoured**, νέκυς σχυμμένος, of Patroclus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.180.}}
**B.II** **to be ashamed, feel shame**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.305, 18.12, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1291.}}
**B.II.2** more commonly, **to be ashamed at** a thing, with _accusative_ _of things_, αἰσχυνόμενοι φάτιν ἀνδρῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.323; τὴν δυσγένειαν τὴν ἐμὴν αἰ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1079: with _dative_ _of things_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 992, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.42, etc.; αἰ. ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.8; ἔν τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.39; περί τινος 33.6. etc.}}
**B.II.2.b** with _participle_, **to be ashamed at** doing a thing (which one does), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 642 (variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 540, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 200, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Grg.” 494e, etc.}}
**B.II.2.c** with _infinitive_, **to be ashamed to** do a thing (and therefore not to do it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 856, “Ch.” 917, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414e, “Phdr.” 257d, etc.}}; though this condition must not be pressed absolutely,{{Refs|compare “Ap.” 22b.}}
**B.II.2.d** followed by _relative_ clause, αἰσχύνεσθαι εἰ.. **to be ashamed** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 254, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.42; ἐάν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.4; μὴ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183e, cf. Machoap. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.579f; ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.23.}}
**B.II.3** with _accusative_ _person_, **to feel shame before** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 934, 1074, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 23.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 216b}}; τοὺς γέροντας (at Sparta) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.180}}; ὅστις γὰρ αὐτὸς αὑτὸν οὐκ αἰσχύνεται, πῶστόν γε μηδὲν εἰδότ᾽ αἰσχυνθήσετα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 229, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.26}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 415; ᾑσχύνθημεν θεοὺς.. προδοῦναι αὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.22; αἰσχύνομαι ὑμᾶς λέγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.48; αἰ. πρός τινα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383b12.}}

154:
** αἰτ-έω**
(_Aeolic dialect_ αἴτημι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 155, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.5)}}, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ αἴτεον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus:}}_future_ αἰτήσω: _aorist_ ᾔτησα: _perfect_ ᾔτηκα{{NT|NT.1John.5.15}}: _pluperfect_ ᾐτήκει{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.15.5:}}_perfect_ _passive_ ᾔτημαι, etc.:—**ask, beg**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.49, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 341.}}
**2** mostly with _accusative_ _of things_, **ask for, demand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.358, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.365, etc.}}; ὁδὸν αἰ. **ask leave** to depart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.17}}; αἰ. τινί τι **to ask** something for one,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|20.74, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.17}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, **ask** a person **for** a thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.295, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.387, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, etc.}}; δίκας αἰ. τινὰ φόνου **to demand** satisfaction from one for.., {{NT+6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.114; αἰ. τι πρός τινος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 556; παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.16; τὰ αἰτήματα ἃ τήκαμεν παρ᾽ αὐτοῦ NT.1John.5.15.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **ask** one **to** do, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.173, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1334, “Ant.” 65, etc.; αἰ. παρά τινος δοῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 398e.}}
**4** with _accusative_ only, **beg of**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.49.}}
**5** in Logic, **postulate, assume**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 41b9 (_passive_), “Top.” 163a6, etc.}}
**II** _middle_, **ask for one's own use, claim**, Λύσανδρον ἄρχοντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.59}}; frequently almost = the _active_, and with the same construct., first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90}} (παρ-),{{Refs 4th c.BC+|9.34, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 822, etc.; αἰτεῖσθαί τινα ὅπως.. Antiphol. 12 codices}}; πάλαισμα μἠποτε λῦσαι θεὸν αἰτοῦμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 880}}; frequently _absolutely_ in _participle_, αἰτουμένῳ μοι δός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 480, compare 2, “Th.” 260, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 63; αἰτουμένη που τεύξεται [prev. author] “Ant.” 778; αἰτησάμενος ἐχρήσατο 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.27; οὐ πῦρ γὰρ αἰτῶν, οὐδὲ λοπάδ᾽ αἰτούμενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 476}}; αἰτεῖσθαι ὑπέρ τινος **to beg** for one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.22.}}
**III** _passive_, of persons, **have a thing begged of** one, αἰτηθέντες χρήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97, etc.; αἰτεύμενος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.63}}: with _infinitive_, **to be asked to** do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).5.}}
**III.2** of things, **to be asked**, τὸ αἰτεόμενον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.112}}; ἵπποι ᾐτημένοι **borrowed** horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.12.}}

155:
** αἴτ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**request, demand**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566b, LXX.1Ki.1.17, NT.Luke.23.24, “PFlor.” 296.16 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** in Logic and in Mathematics texts, **postulate, assumption**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 74.}}

156:
** αἰτί-α**, ἡ,
**responsibility**, mostly in bad sense, **guilt, blame**, or the imputation thereof, i.e. **accusation**, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.35 and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, see below (8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses αἴτιος):}} —Phrases: αἰτίαν ἔχειν bear **responsibility for**, τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 579, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1312}}; but usually **to be accused**, τινός **of** a crime, φόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.70}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 506}}; followed by ὡς.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38c}}; by ὡς with _participle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 249d}}; ὑπό τινος **by** some one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 99, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565b: reversely, αἰτία ἔχει τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.70, 71; αἰ. φεύγειν τινός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1404}}; ἐν αἰτίᾳ εἶναι or γίγνεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 67, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.6}}; αἰτίαν ὑπέχειν lie under **a charge**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 33b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.16; ὑπομεῖναι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.139; φέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60}}; λαβεῖν ἀπό τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 18; αἰτίαις ἐνέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52a; αἰτίαις περιπίπτειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.1; εἰς αἰτίαν ἐμπίπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 150a; αἰτίας τυγχάνειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.2; ἐκτὸς αἰτίας κυρεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 332}}; ἐν αἰτίῃ ἔχειν hold one **guilty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106; δι᾽ αἰτίας ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, etc.; ἐν αἰτίᾳ βάλλειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 656}}; τὴν αἰτίαν ἐπιφέρειν τινί impute **the fault** to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26; αἰτίαν νέμειν τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 28; ἐπάγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283; προσβάλλειν τινί 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.4}}; ἀνατιθέναι, προστιθέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 21, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 640, etc.}}; ἀπολύειν τινὰ τῆς αἰτίης to acquit of **guilt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.88, etc.}}
**2** in forensic oratory, **invective** without proof (opposed to ἔλεγχος), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.23, compare 18.15.}}
**3** in good sense, εἰ.. εὖ πράξαιμεν, αἰτία θεοῦ **the credit** is his, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 4}}; δι᾽ ὅντινα αἰτίαν ἔχουσιν Ἀθηναῖοι βελτίους γεγονέναι **are reputed** to have become better, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 503b, cf. Alcaeus1.119a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 984b19}}; ὧν.. πέρι αἰτίαν ἔχεις διαφέρειν in which you **are reputed** to excel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169a}}; οἳ.. ἔχουσι ταύτην τὴν αἰ. who have this **reputation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 435e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.12; αἰτίαν λαμβάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 624a.}}
**4** **expostulation**, μὴ ἐπ᾽ ἔχθρᾳ τὸ πλέον ἢ αἰτίᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69.}}
**II** **cause**, δι᾽ ἣν αἰτίην ἐπολέμησαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Prooem.”, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 83, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68e, “Phd.” 97a}} {αἰτιάζομαι}, etc.; on the four causes of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus see “Ph.” 194b16, “Metaphorical” 983a26:—αἰ. τοῦ γενέσθαι or γεγονέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97a; τοῦ μεγίστου ἀγαθοῦ τῇ πόλει αἰτία ἡ κοινωνία [prev. author] “R.” 464b:}} — _dative_ αἰτίᾳ **for the sake of**, κοινοῦ τινος ἀγαθοῦ[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.29:—αἴτιον compare (αἴτιος 11.2) is used like{αἰτία}}}] in the sense of **cause**, not in that of **accusation.**
**III** **occasion, motive**, αἰτίαν ῥοαῖσι Μοισᾶν ἐνέβαλε gave them **a the me** for song, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.11; αἰτίαν παρέχειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 13.}}
**IV** **head, category** under which a thing comes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.75.}}
**V** **case in dispute**, ἡ αἰ. τοῦ ἀνθρώπου μετὰ τῆς γυναικός{{NT|NT.Matt.10.10.}}

158:
** αἴτι-ος**, α, ον,
more rarely ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 547: (see. αἰτία)}}:— **culpable, responsible**, ἐπεὶ οὔ τί μοι αἴτιοί εἰσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.153, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.34, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.214}}: _comparative_ αἰτιώτερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20: _superlative_, τοὺς αἰτιωτάτους}} **the most guilty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.50; αἰ. τινος [prev. author] 3.52.}}
**2** _substantive_, αἴτιος, ὁ, **the accused, the culprit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 70, etc.}}; οἱ αἴ. τοῦ πατρός **they who have sinned against** my father, {{Refs|[prev. work] 273}}:—with _genitive_ _of things_, οἱ αἴ. τοῦ φόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 117, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 590, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200.}}
**II** **responsible for**, with _genitive_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, etc.}}; αἴτιός τινός τινι being **the cause of** a thing **to** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.57, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.100}}: with _infinitive_, αἴ. τὸν ἠέρα ξηρὸν εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26; τοῦ μὴ φαλακροῦσθαι [prev. author] 3.12, etc.; αἴ. θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1173; αἰ. πεμφθῆναι ἄγγελον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.23, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.82; αἴτιος τὸ σὲ ἀποκρίνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 190e}}: _comparative_, τοῦ.. ἐλευθέραν εἶναι.. αἰτιώτερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.5, compare 51.21: _superlative_ αἰτιώτατος, ἐν τῷ στενῷ ναυμαχῆσαι}} **mainly instrumental in causing** the sea-fight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; αἰ. τοῦ μὴ ἀποθανεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.42; -ώτατόν τι καὶ τελέως δραστικόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.23.}}
**II.2** αἴτιον, τό, **cause**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 6 (_plural_), 21, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.125, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 939, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, etc.}}; τί ποτ᾽ οὖν ἐστι τὸ αἴτιον τό.. μηδένα εἰπεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.56; frequently in Philos., τὸ δ᾽ αἴ. τούτου εἶναι ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 11oe, etc.}}

159:
** αἴτι-ος**, α, ον,
more rarely ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 547: (see. αἰτία)}}:— **culpable, responsible**, ἐπεὶ οὔ τί μοι αἴτιοί εἰσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.153, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.34, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.214}}: _comparative_ αἰτιώτερος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20: _superlative_, τοὺς αἰτιωτάτους}} **the most guilty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.50; αἰ. τινος [prev. author] 3.52.}}
**2** _substantive_, αἴτιος, ὁ, **the accused, the culprit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 70, etc.}}; οἱ αἴ. τοῦ πατρός **they who have sinned against** my father, {{Refs|[prev. work] 273}}:—with _genitive_ _of things_, οἱ αἴ. τοῦ φόνου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 117, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 590, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200.}}
**II** **responsible for**, with _genitive_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, etc.}}; αἴτιός τινός τινι being **the cause of** a thing **to** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.57, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.100}}: with _infinitive_, αἴ. τὸν ἠέρα ξηρὸν εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26; τοῦ μὴ φαλακροῦσθαι [prev. author] 3.12, etc.; αἴ. θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1173; αἰ. πεμφθῆναι ἄγγελον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.23, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.82; αἴτιος τὸ σὲ ἀποκρίνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 190e}}: _comparative_, τοῦ.. ἐλευθέραν εἶναι.. αἰτιώτερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.5, compare 51.21: _superlative_ αἰτιώτατος, ἐν τῷ στενῷ ναυμαχῆσαι}} **mainly instrumental in causing** the sea-fight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74; αἰ. τοῦ μὴ ἀποθανεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.42; -ώτατόν τι καὶ τελέως δραστικόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.23.}}
**II.2** αἴτιον, τό, **cause**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 6 (_plural_), 21, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.125, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 939, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, etc.}}; τί ποτ᾽ οὖν ἐστι τὸ αἴτιον τό.. μηδένα εἰπεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.56; frequently in Philos., τὸ δ᾽ αἴ. τούτου εἶναι ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 11oe, etc.}}

161:
** αἰχμᾰλ-ωσία**, ἡ,
**captivity**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.61, LXX.Amos.1.15, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 31.}}
**II** **body of captives**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.70, LXX.Num.31.12, +others}}

164:
** αἰχμᾰλ-ωτος**, ον,
**taken by the spear, captive, prisoner**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 223, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.79, 134}}; frequently of women, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1440, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 417:}} — αἰχμάλωτοι **prisoners of war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.22, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70}}; αἰ. λαμβάνειν, ἄγειν take **prisoner**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.37, 4.4.1}}; αἰ. γίγνεσθαι to be **taken**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.1.7}}; of things, αἰ. χρήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 400, compare “Ag.” 334, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.139; νῆες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.8, “IG” 2.789}}; τὰ αἰ. **booty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.26, “An.” 4.1.13}}; αἰχμάλωτον, τό, ={ἀνδράποδον}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.57.}}
**II** ={αἰχμαλωτικός, δουλοσύνη αἰ}. **such as awaits a captive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76; εὐνά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 364 (Lyric poetry); τύχη 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 27.6, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.157.}}
**III** αἰχμάλωτος, ὁ, name of plasters, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.20.}}

165:
** αἰών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἡ, as in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.186, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1484:}} _apocopation_ _accusative_ αἰῶ], like{Ποσειδῶ}, restored by Ahrens (from {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 363) in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 350: (properly αἰϝών}}, compare **aevum**, see at {αἰεί}):—**period of existence** (τὸ τέλος τὸ περιέχον τὸν τῆς ἑκάστου ζωῆς χρόνον.. αἰὼν ἑκάστου κέκληται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 279a25):}}
**I** **lifetime, life**, ψυχή τε καὶ αἰών{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.453; ἐκ δ᾽ αἰ. πέφαται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.27; μηδέ τοι αἰ. φθινέτω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.160; λείπει τινά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.685}}; ἀπ᾽ αἰῶνος νέος ὤλεο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus νέον) 24.725; τελευτᾶν τὸν αἰῶνα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.; αἰῶνος στερεῖν τινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 862; αἰῶνα διοιχνεῖν [prev. author] “Eu.” 315; συνδιατρίβειν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 1}}; αἰ. Αἰακιδᾶν, _periphrastic_ for **the Aeacidae**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 645 assuming variant; ἀπέπνευσεν αἰῶνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 801; ἐμὸν κατ᾽ αἰῶνα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 219.}}
**I.2** **age, generation**, αἰ. ἐς τρίτον {{Refs|[prev. work] 744}}; ὁ μέλλων αἰών **posterity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.199, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 370c.}}
**I.3** **one's life, destiny, lot**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 34, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1215, “fragment” 30, etc.}}
**II** **long space of time, age**, αἰὼν γίγνεται 'tis **an age**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 536.5}}; especially with Preps., ἀπ᾽ αἰῶνος **of old**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 609, NT.Luke.1.70; οἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ αἰ. Ῥωμαῖοι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.20}}; δι᾽ αἰῶνος **perpetually**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 26, “Eu.” 563}}; **all one's life long**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1024}}; δι᾽ αἰῶνος μακροῦ, ἀπαύστου, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 582, 574}}; τὸν δι᾽ αἰ. χρόνον **for ever**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 554}}; εἰς ἅπαντα τὸν αἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 106, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.62}}; εἰς τὸν αἰ. {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Gen.3.23, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 21.17, NT.John.8.35, Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 17; εἰς αἰῶνα αἰῶνος LXX.Psa.131(132).14}}; ἐξ αἰῶνος καὶ ἕως αἰῶνος{{LXX|LXX.Jer.7.7}}; ἐπ᾽ αἰ.{{LXX|LXX.Exo.15.18}}; ἕως αἰῶνος{{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.1.22, +others:— without a _preposition_, τὸν ἅπαντα αἰ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 279a22; τὸν αἰῶνα 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 62, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.8U.}}; **eternity**, opposed to χρόνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37d, compare 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 37, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.496, 619, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.5, etc.; τοὺς ὑπὲρ τοῦ αἰῶνος φόβους 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 20.}}
**II.2** **space of time** clearly defined and marked out, **epoch, age**, ὁ αἰὼν οὗτος this present **world**, opposed to ὁ μέλλων, {{NT|NT.Matt.13.22, cf. NT.Rom.12.2; ὁ νῦν αἰ. NT.1Tim.6.17, NT.2Tim.4.10}}:—hence in _plural_, **the ages**, i.e. **eternity**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 84; εἰς πάντας τοὺς αἰ. LXX.Tob.13.4}}; εἰς τοὺς αἰ{{LXX+NT|[prev. work]. LXX.Sir.45.24, +others, NT.Rom.1.25, etc.; εἰς τοὺς αἰ. τῶν αἰώνων LXX.4Mac.18.24, NT.Phil.4.20, etc.}}; ἀπὸ τῶν αἰ., πρὸ τῶν αἰ., {{NT|NT.Eph.3.9, 1 “Cor.” 2.7; τὰ τέλη τῶν αἰ. [prev. work]10.11.}}
**II.3** Αἰών, ὁ, personified, Αἰὼν Χρόνου παῖς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 900 (Lyric poetry), compare “Corpus Hermeticum” 11, etc.}}; as title of various divine beings, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 151, +others; especially=Persian Zervan, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Ἡρασκος}.}}]
**II.4** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus, = 10, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 59.}}
**B** **spinal marrow** (perhaps regarded as seat of **life), _Hymns of _Merc** 42, 119, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 111, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.122; perhaps also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.27.}}

166:
** αἰώνιος**, ον,
also α, ον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37d, NT.Heb.9.12}}:— **lasting for an age** (αἰών 11), **perpetual, eternal** (but distinct from ἀΐδιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.3), μέθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363d; ἀνώλεθρον.. ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ αἰώνιον [prev. author] “Lg.” 904a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 28; αἰ. κατὰ ψυχὴν ὄχλησις [prev. author] “Nat.” 131 G.}}; κακά, δεινά, {{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.18, “D.” 1.13; αἰ. ἀμοιβαῖς βασανισθησόμενοι [prev. work]19; τοῦ αἰ. θεοῦ NT.Rom.16.26, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96c; οὐ χρονίη μοῦνον.. ἀλλ᾽ αἰωνίη 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.5}}; αἰ. διαθήκη, νόμιμον, πρόσταγμα, {{LXX+NT+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.9.16, LXX.Exo.27.21, LXX.Tob.1.6; ζωή NT.Matt.25.46, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.20}}; κόλασις {{NT+6th c.AD+|NT.Matt.25.46, 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Platonis Gorgiam commentaria - Neue Jahrbücher für Philologie, Suppl.” p.278J.; πρὸ χρόνων αἰ. NT.2Tim.1.9: opposed to πρόσκαιρος, NT.2Cor.4.18.}}
**2** **holding an office** or **title for life, perpetual**, γυμνασίαρχος{{Refs|“CPHerm.” 62.}}
**3** = Latin _saecularis_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phlegon Trallianus Paradoxographus “Macr.” 4.}}
**4** _adverb_ -ίως **eternally**, νοῦς ἀκίνητος αἰ. πάντα ὤν{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 172, compare 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.77D.}}; **perpetually**, μισεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 338.}}
**5** αἰώνιον, τό, ={ἀείζωον τὸ μέγα}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.88.}}

167:
** ἀκᾰθαρ-σία**, ἡ,
**uncleanness, foulness**, of a wound or sore, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72c(_plural_); ἀγγείων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.31.}}
**b** **dirt, filth**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1117.27 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**2** in moral sense, **depravity**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119.}}
**3** **ceremonial impurity**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.15.3, +others}}

168:
Related to: ** ἀκᾰθαρ-τος**, ον,
(καθαίρω) **uncleansed, foul**, ἀήρ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6}} of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 883b27; ἕλκος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 27}}; of a woman, **quae menstrua non habet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator “Fragmenta - Orat.” _Attic dialect_ 4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 19}}; of ceremonial impurity, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.12.2, +others, “IG” 3.74.3.}}
**b** **unpurified**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 866a, 868a.}}
**2** **morally unclean, impure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.199, etc.; ἀκάθαρτε}}**thou beast!** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 5}}; ={μανιώδης}, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 30; ἀ. πνεῦμα LXX.Zech.13.2, NT.Matt.12.43, compare “PMag.Par.” 1.1238}}. _adverb_ ἀκαθάρτως, ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 92b.}}
**3** ofthings, **notpurgedaway, unpurged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 256, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854b.}}
**3.b** **unpruned**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.15.1.}}
**3.c** **ceremonially unclean**, of food, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.5.2, +others, NT.Act.10.14.}}
**3.d** **not sifted, containing impurities,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.8 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _active_, **not fit for cleansing**, [φάρμακα] ἑλκέων ἀκαθαρτότερα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.8.}}

169:
** ἀκᾰθαρ-τος**, ον,
(καθαίρω) **uncleansed, foul**, ἀήρ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6}} of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 883b27; ἕλκος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 27}}; of a woman, **quae menstrua non habet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demades Orator “Fragmenta - Orat.” _Attic dialect_ 4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 19}}; of ceremonial impurity, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.12.2, +others, “IG” 3.74.3.}}
**b** **unpurified**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 866a, 868a.}}
**2** **morally unclean, impure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.199, etc.; ἀκάθαρτε}}**thou beast!** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 5}}; ={μανιώδης}, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 30; ἀ. πνεῦμα LXX.Zech.13.2, NT.Matt.12.43, compare “PMag.Par.” 1.1238}}. _adverb_ ἀκαθάρτως, ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 92b.}}
**3** ofthings, **notpurgedaway, unpurged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 256, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 854b.}}
**3.b** **unpruned**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.15.1.}}
**3.c** **ceremonially unclean**, of food, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.5.2, +others, NT.Act.10.14.}}
**3.d** **not sifted, containing impurities,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.8 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _active_, **not fit for cleansing**, [φάρμακα] ἑλκέων ἀκαθαρτότερα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.8.}}

170:
** ἀκαιρ-έω**,
**to be without an opportunity**, opposed to εὐκαιρέω, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10.7}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ ἠκαιρεῖσθε{{NT|NT.Phil.4.10.}}
**2** **talk nonsense**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 74.9.}}

171:
Included with: ** ἄκαιρος**, ον,
**ill-timed, unseasonable**, ἐς ἄκαιρα πονεῖν{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 919; οὐκ ἄκαιρα λέγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1036; ἄ. κένωσις 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10; προθυμία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65; ἐλευθερία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 569c; ἔπαινος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 24ce; ῥᾳθυμία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.46, γέλως 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 88}}. _adverb_ **-ρως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 808, “Ch.” 624 (both Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 17, +others}}: _comparative_ **-οτέρως**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 1.19}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, ἄκαιρ᾽ ἀπώλλυτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1081.}}
**II** of persons, **importunate, troublesome**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 12; ἄ. καὶ λάλος 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.62.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **ill-suited** to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 7.6 (_comparative_).}}
**III** ἄκαιρον, τό, ={μυρσίνη ἀγρία}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.144.}}

172:
** ἄκᾰκ-ος**, ον,
**unknowing of ill, guileless**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 663 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91d, NT.Rom.16.18.}}
**2** **innocent, simple** (compare εὐήθης), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.46, 82; ἄ. ἀνθρώπων τρόπος 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 33}}. _adverb_ **-κως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.50.}}
**II** **unharmed**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 149.}}
**II.2** **unadulterated,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 142 (6th c.AD).}}

173:
** ἄκανθ-α** [ᾰκ], ης, ἡ,
(ἀκή A) **thorn, prickle**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 655a19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.1.3: hence}}
**1** **any thorny** or **prickly plant** (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.328 (_plural_)}} probably **Eryngium campestre**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 718, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 107.19, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.132, etc.: proverbial, οὐ γὰρ ἄκανθαι}} no **thistles**, i.e. 'an easy job', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 272, 483:}} —special kinds: ἄ. Ἀραβική **smaller milk-thistle, Notobasis syriaca**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.13}}; ἄ. βασιλική **fish-thistle, Cnicus Acarna**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.10.5}}; ἄ. Ἰνδική, = **Balsamodendron Mukul**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HP” 9.1.2}}; ἄ. λευκή **Acacia albida**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.2.8}}; ={ἄ. βασιλική}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.12}}; ἄ. λευκὴ τρίοζος, = **Euphorbia antiquorum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.12}}; ἄ. ἀκανώδης (probably) **cornthistle, Carduus arvensis**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 10.6.}}
**2** of other plants, e.g. **Spanish broom, Spartium junceum**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.5.10:— ={ἀκακία, ἄ. Αἰγυπτία}, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.1.2. compare “POxy.” 1188.10 (13 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator), etc.}}]; ἄ. μέλαινα **Acacia arabica**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.2.8, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96, 2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Isa.41.19}}; ἄ. διψάς, = **Acacia tortilis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.7.1.}}
**3** central flowering-bud of χαμαιλέων λευκός,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 9.12.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.8.}}
**4** ={ἄκανθος}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.17.}}
**5** in _plural_, **prickles** or **spines** of the hedgehog and of certain fish, Ion Trag.38, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 530b8.}}
**6** **backbone** or **spine** of fish, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 275, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 969, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110.11, +others}}; of serpents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.75, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.32, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.150}}; of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 492, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 654a26, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.451, etc.}}; improperly used of mammalia, _accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.98a22}}; of **the spinous processes of the vertebrae**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.758}}; χονδρώδεις ἄ. false ribs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Oss.” 25.}}
**7** _metaphorically_ in _plural_, **thorny** questions, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hes.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.97d.}}

174:
** ἀκάνθ-ινος**, η, ον,
**of thorns**, στέφανος{{LXX+NT|NT.Mark.15.17, LXX.Josh.19.5.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **thorny**, ἐν ἀ. ἀταρποῖς{{Refs|“Anacreontea” 53.12.}}
**II** **of shittah-wood**, ἱστός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96; ξύλα “PLond.” 3.1177.191 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ ἀ. cloths **made of** ἀκάνθιον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|2, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.5.10.}}
**II.2** ἀ. πάππος **thistle-down**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.81.}}

175:
** ἄκαρπ-ος**, ον,
**without fruit, barren**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 898.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91c}}; ἄ. ξύλον, ={ἀκακία}, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Isa.41.19: with _genitive_, λίμνη ἄ. ἰχθύων 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.7.3.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **fruitless, unprofitable**, πόνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 7.5; λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277a; τὰ ἄ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1125a11}}. _adverb_ **-πως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 254.}}
**II** _active_, **making barren**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 942, compare 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus Tyr. 5.4.}}

180:
** ἀκατά-παυστος**, ον,
**not to be set at rest, incessant**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.17.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.67, etc.}}; **that cannotceasefrom**, τινός{{NT|NT.2Pet.2.14.}}_adverb_ -τως{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1001.}}
**II** **not to be checked, irresistible,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.2364.}}

181:
** ἀκατα-στᾰσία**, ἡ,
**instability, anarchy, confusion,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|Stoic. 3.99, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.70.1, 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus “Vita Caesaris” 28, etc.: _plural_, LXX.Pro.26.28, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.31, NT.2Cor.6.5.}}
**II** **unsteadiness**, τοῦ σώματος{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.121; ἀ. καὶ μανία 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.4.8: _plural_, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 5.57.}}

182:
** ἀκατά-στᾰτος**, ον,
(καθίστημι) **unstable, unsettled**, καιροί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.8; πνεῦμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.136, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 941b29}}; **disorderly**, ὁρμαί{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.166; πολιτεία 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.74}}:—of men, **fickle**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.4.6}}; of fevers, **irregular**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut. (Sp.)” 20.}}_adverb_ -ως, ἔχειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 21.7.}}
**II** **not making any deposit**, οὖρον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.32.}}

185:
** ἀκέραι-ος**, ον
(_feminine_ -αία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 593), Prose word (used by 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, see below) for poetry ἀκήρατος}}, **pure, unmixed**, ὕδωρ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 605a15; οἶνος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.6; ἀργύριον 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.86, etc.}}; **untouched**, γῆ, νομή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 575b3}}; **unalloyed**, ἡδοναί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 12.}}
**2** of persons, **pure in blood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 943.}}
**II** **unharmed, unravaged**, ἀ. ἀπολαμβάνειν τὴν πόλιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146; γῆ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18; χώρα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.28}}; δύναμις, of an army, **in full force**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3}}; of troops, **fresh**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.9, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.40.12, etc.}}; of property, **untouched**, οὐσία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.23}}; ἐᾶν τι ἀσινὲς καὶ ἀ. {{Refs|“IG” 3.1418f}}; of a person, Persae.{{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.99.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **pure, inviolate**, ἀκέραιον ὡς σῴσαιμι Μενέλεῳ λέχος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 48}}; [τέχνη] ἀβλαβὴς καὶ ἀ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 342b}}; **complete, perfect**, φαντασίαι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.8; ἐλπίς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.9.3; ὁρμαί [prev. author] 1.45.2.}}
**II.3** of persons, **uncontaminated, guileless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 922}}; **incorruptible**, κριτής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.4: with _genitive_, ἀ. κακῶν ἠθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 489}}; **unprejudiced, with an open mind**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.31.12.}}
**II.4** ἐξ ἀκεραίου **anew**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.4.10}}; **while matters are undecided**, {{Refs|Idd. 6.24.9}}; ἀκέραιον ἐᾶν leave **alone**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.2.10}}; εἰς -ον ἀποκαθιστάναι, = Latin _in integrum restituere,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.951.}}_adverb_ -ως, of payment, **in full**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 15.21.2}}; **unreservedly**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.57O.}}

186:
** ἀκλῐνής**, ές,
**lending to neither side, unswerving**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109a; πρὸς τὸ δίκαιον Nic.Dam. p.144D.}}; **impartial**, ἀκοαί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|“POxy.” 904.9 (V 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator)}}; **regular**, ἀκλινέων καλάμων{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 10.11 (1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus)}}, etc.: in Mathematics texts, **without inclination**, of a perpendicular, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.132F.}}; **horizontal**, of a plane, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 1048.2, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Dioptra” 19: Musicius Epigrammaticus}}, **fixed**, of notes in scale, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD(?): Alypius Musicus 4}}: in Medicine texts, **persistent**, πυρετοί{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.20.24}}. _adverb_ **-νῶς**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.669}}; _Ionic dialect_ **-νέως**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.54 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **steadfast, steady**, φιλία{{NT+1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 12.158 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ὁμολογία NT.Heb.10.23; ψυχή 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 33}}; **unmoved, tranquil**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 35.11, +others}}

187:
** ἀκμ-άζω**, ἀκμή
) **to be in full bloom, at the prime**:
**I** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335e}}; ἀ. σώματι, ῥώμῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310d, etc.}}; of cities and states, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57, 5.28; ἀ. τὸ σῶμα ἀπὸ τῶν λ ἐτῶν μέχρι τῶν έ καὶ λ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b9}}; ={τὰ τῶν νέων πράττειν} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 122.}}
**I.2** **flourish, abound in** a thing, πλούτῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29; παρασκευῇ πάσῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1; νεότητι [prev. author] 2.20; ναυσὶκαὶ χρήμασι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.163.}}
**I.3** with _infinitive_, **to be strong enough** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.25.}}
**II** of things, ἀ. ὁ πυρετός, ἡ νόσος **is at its height**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.29, “Epid.” 1.25, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; τοῦ πάθους ἀκμάζοντος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.31 O.; ἀ. ὁ πόλεμος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3}}; of corn, **to be ripe**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.19.}}
**II.2** ἡνίκα.. ἀκμάζοι [ὁ θυμός] when passion **is at its height**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 70d; ἀκμάζουσα ῥώμη 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.3}}; ἀκμάζει πάντα ἐπιμελείας δεόμενα require **the utmost** care, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.40.}}
**II.3** _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, ἀκμάζει βρετέων ἔχεσθαι '**tis time** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 97 (Lyric poetry)}}; νῦν γὰρ ἀ. Πειθὼ.. ξυγκαταβῆναι now '**tis time** for her to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 726.}}

188:
** ἀκμήν**,
_accusative_ of ἀκμή, used as _adverb_, **as yet, still**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 451 G, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus in “Codex Vat.Gr.” 122; un-Attic _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 100, but compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 116}}; τὰ σκευοφόρα.. ἀκμὴν διέβαινε were **just** crossing the river, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.26, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.13.12, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.60, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.141 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.29 W., NT.Matt.15.16, etc.; νέος ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.164; strengthened, ἀκμὴν ἔτι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.4.9, 15.6.6; ἔτι ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.26.}}
**II** ={ἀκμαίως}, {{Refs|Cratin.in “Codex Vat.Gr.” 122}}: perhaps, = **much**, {{Refs|“OGI” 201.13 (from Nubia).}}

189:
** ἀκοή**, ἡ,
_Epic dialect_ ἀκουή· (ἀκοϝ, compare ἀκούω):— **hearing, sound heard**, ἕκαθεν δέ τε γίγνετ᾽ ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.634.}}
**2** **thing heard, tidings**, μετὰ πατρὸς ἀκουὴν ἱκέσθαι, βῆναι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.308, 4.701}}; κατὰ τὴν Σόλωνος ἀκοήν according to Solon's **story**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21a}}; **report**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.84, 90}}; ἀ. σοφοῖς **thing** for wise men **to listen to**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.78}}; ἀκοῇ ἱστορεῖν, παραλαβεῖν τι **by hearsay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, 148; ἐπίστασθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.67, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126; ἐξ ἀκοῆς λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61d}}; τὰς ἀ. τῶν προγεγενημένων **traditions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20; ἀκοαὶ.. λόγων [prev. author] 1.73}}; ἀκοὴν μαρτυρεῖν, προσάγειν, give, bring **hearsay evidence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.4; βαρὺν.. ἀκοῆς ψόφον “Anthologia Graeca” 6.220 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐκ γὰρ ἀκουῆς οἰκτίρω σε{{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work] 7.220 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** **sense of hearing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38, etc.}}; joined with ὄψις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65b, etc.; οἷς ὦτα μέν ἐστιν, ἀκοαὶδὲ οὐκ ἔνεισιν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.474.}}
**II.2** **act of hearing**, ἐς ἀκοὰν ἐμήν to my **hearing**, my **ear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 689; γᾶρυν ἀραρεῖν ἀκοαῖσι 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 41; ὀξεῖαν ἀ... λόγοις διδούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 30; ἀκοῇ κλύειν [prev. author] “Ph.” 1412}}; ἀκοαῖς δέχεσθαι, εἰς ἀκοὰς.. ἥκειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1496, “Ph.” 1480; δι᾽ ἀκοῆς αἰσθάνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 900a}}; ἀκοὴν ὑπειπών demanding **a hearing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 962; τοῖς ἀκροάμασι τὰς ἀ. ἀνατεθεικώς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.5.9.}}
**II.3** **ear**, ὀππάτεσσι δ᾽ οὖδεν ὄρημ᾽, ἐπιρρόμβεισι δ᾽ ἄκουαι{{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.12, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.17; ἀπεσθίει μου τὴν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 52, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 199}}; δυσὶν ἀκοαῖς κρίνειν with two **ears**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b27, compare “Pr.” 960a30, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 106.5.}}
**III** **hearing, listening to**, ἀκοῆς ἄξιος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142d}}; εἰς ἀκοὴν φωνῆς within **hearing** of.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.41.}}
**IV** **obedience**, ἀ. ὑπὲρ θυσίαν ἀγαθή{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.15.22.}}
**V** in _plural_, place where **supernatural voices are heard,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.955.10 (Epid.), 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 32; αἱ ἀ. τοῦ θεοῦ 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 47(23).13.}}

190:
** ἀκολουθ-έω**,
**follow** one, **go after** or **with** him, frequently of soldiers and slaves:—mostly with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 19, etc.; ἀ. τῷ ἡγουμένῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474c; with Preps., ἀ. μετά τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187e, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.27, etc.; τοῖς σώμασι μετ᾽ ἐκείνων ἠκολούθουν, ταῖς δ᾽ εὐνοίαις μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν ἦσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.15; ἀ. σύν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.3; κατόπιν τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 13: rarely with _accusative_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 558: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 18.8, etc.}}; ἀ. ἐφ᾽ ἁρπαγήν, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.98}}; ἀκολουθῶν, ὁ, as _substantive_, ={ἀκόλουθος}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Adul.fragment” 1.}}
**2** of stars, **follow** in the diurnal rotation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 2.2.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **follow, be guided by**, τῇ γνώμῃ τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; τοῖς πράγμασιν, τοῖς τοῦ πολέμου καιροῖς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.39, 24.95}}; **obey**, τοῖς νόμοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.19: with _accusative_ _neuter_, ἀ. ἅπαντα “PLille” 1.26.}}
**II.2** **follow the thread** of a discourse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107b, etc.}}
**II.3** of things, **follow upon, to be consequent upon, consistent with**, εὐλογία.. εὐηθείᾳ ἀ.{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 400e, compare 398d}}; **follow analogy of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499a10, +others}}
**II.3.b** _absolutely_, **to be consequent**, ὡς γένους ὄντος τοῦ ἀεὶ ἀκολουθοῦντος{{Refs|“Top.” 128b4}}; as species to individual, {{Refs|“GA” 768b13.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, ἀκολουθεῖ **it follows**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cat.” 14a31.—Not in Trag.: first in 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 55, with ᾱ (assuming variant)}}, elsewhere ; takes place of ἕπομαι in later Greek.

191:
** ἀκούω**:
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ᾰκουον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.442:}}_future_ ἀκούσομαι (_active_ ἀκούσω first in {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 34 assuming variant, then in 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 378, 686, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.57, NT.Matt.12.19, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἤκουσα, _Epic dialect_ ᾰκουσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.223:}}_perfect_ ἀκήκοα, _Laconian dialect_ ἄκουκα{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 20, “Ages.” 21}}; ἤκουκα is a late form, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 vii 23 (2nd c.AD)}}; later _Ionic dialect_ ἀκήκουκα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.49}}: _pluperfect_ ἀκηκόειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.52, 7.208; ἠκηκόειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.7}}; old _Attic dialect_ ἠκηκόη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 800, “Pax” 616, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384b:}} —rare in _middle_, _present_ (see. below {{Refs|11.2)}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀκούετο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.331}}: _aorist_ ἠκουσάμην{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.119}}:—_passive_, _future_ ἀκουσθήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507d}}: _aorist_ ἠκούσθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 5}}: _perfect_ ἤκουσμαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 11.10, Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 4}}; ἀκήκουσμαι is uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 49}}: _pluperfect_ ἤκουστο{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anonymus cited in 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 217}}, (παρ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.10.10. (ἀ-κοϝ-, compare κοέω)}}:—**hear**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: properly with _accusative_ of thing heard, _genitive_ of person from whom it is heard, ταῦτα Καλυψοῦς ἤκουσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.389, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 43, etc.; _genitive_ _person_ frequently omitted, πάντ᾽ ἀκήκοας λόγον [prev. author] “Aj.” 480, etc.}}; or the _accusative_ _of things_, ἄκουε τοῦ θανόντος{{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 792, compare 793}}:—also with _genitive_ _of things_, φθογγῆς, κτύπου, **hear** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.198 (as see l.), 21.237; λόγων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1187; once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in _middle_, ἀκούετο λαὸς ἀϋτῆς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.331.}}
**b** with _genitive_ objecti, **hear of, hear tell of**, ἀ. πατρός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.114: frequently with}}_participle_, τεθνηῶτος (i.e. πατρός) ἀκούσῃς{{Refs|1.289, etc.}}; but εἰ.. πατρὸς νόστον ἀ.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 287; ἀ. περί τινος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.270, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 964, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358d, 358e; τι περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.30.}}
**c** in Prose the _person_ from whom thing is heard frequently takes _preposition_, ἀ. τι ἀπό, ἐκ, παρά, πρός τινος, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.524, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 7, 95, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.125.}}
**d** less frequently with _double_ _genitive_ _person_ et _of things_, **hear of** a thing **from** a person, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.115, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.9.}}
**e** with _participle_ or _infinitive_ added, as εἰ πτώσσοντας ὑφ᾽ Ἕκτορι πάντας ἀκούσαι **should he hear** that all are now crouching under Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.129, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.θ, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.12, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.9}}; ἀ. αὐτὸν ὄλβιον εἶναι **to hear [generally**] that he is happy, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.543, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.13, etc.:—also ἀ. τινὰ ὅτι or ὡς, Ἀτρεΐδην ἀκοετε ὡς.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.193}}; τὸν Δαίδαλον οὐκ ἀκήκοας, ὁτι..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.33; ἀ. οὕνεκα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 33.}}
**f** with _genitive_ et _participle_, to express **what one actually hears** from a person, ταῦτ᾽.. ἤκουον σαφῶς Ὀδυσσέως λέγοντος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 595}}; ἀ. τινὸς λέγοντος, διαλεγομένου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.1}}: rarely with _accusative_ et _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 614.}}
**2** **know by hearsay**, ἔξοιδ᾽ ἀκούων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 105}}: _present_ is used like a _perfect_, νῆσός τις Συρίη κικλήσκεται, εἴ που ἀκούεις{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.403, compare 3.193}}; in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 503c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 13.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **hearken, give ear**, especially in proclamations, ἀκούετε λεῴ **oyez! oyez!** Susar.[{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, etc.: for 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1386 see at {πηγή} 2.}}]
**4** οἱ ἀκούοντες **readers of a book**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.13.6, +others}}
**II** **listen to, give ear to**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.381, etc.: _metaphorically_, Φωκυλίδου οὐκ ἀκούει; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407a: rarely with _dative_, ἀ. ἀνέρι κηδομένῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.515 (in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 227 τίνι is Eth. _dative_): with _genitive_ of}}_participle_ after _dative_, ὅττι οἱ ὦκ᾽ ἤκουσε.. θεὸς εὐξαμένοιο{{Refs|[prev. work] 531.}}
**II.2** **obey**, βασιλῆος, θεοῦ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.256, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.11:}} —_middle_, Λεωφίλου δ᾽ ἀκούεται [πάντα] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 64.}}
**II.3** **hear and understand**, κλύοντες οὐκ ἤκουον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 448, compare “Ch.” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1173; τὸ μὴ πάντας πάντων ἀκούειν 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.37.}}
**II.4** **to be a pupil of**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.21.}}
**III** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, serving as}}_passive_ to λέγειν, **hear oneself called, be called**, like Latin _audire_, εἴπερ ὄρθ᾽ ἀκούεις, Ζεῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 903 (compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 161)}}; frequently with εὖ and κακῶς, κακῶς ἀ. ὑπό τινος **to be ill spoken of by** one; πρός τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16}}.; περί τινος for a thing, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.86}}.; ἄμεινον, ἄριστα ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173, 8.93, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1313, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.75, etc.}}
**III.2** with _nominative_ of subject, ἀκούειν κακός, καλός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 988, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 207a; νῦν κόλακες καὶ θεοῖς ἐχθροὶ.. ἀκούουσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.46, etc.; ἔχαιρε ἀκούων Αἰετὸς ὁ Πύρρος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.45}}; later in _passive_ in this sense, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 21.220, +others}}
**III.3** with _infinitive_, ἤκουον εἶναι πρῶτοι **were said** to be first, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131; also ἀκούσομαι μὲν ὡς ἔφυν οἴκτου πλέως 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1074.}}
**III.4** with _accusative_ _of things_, ἀ. κακά **have** evil **spoken of** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 388, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 607; ἀ. λόγον ἐσλόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).13; φήμας.. κακὰς ἤκουσεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 615.}}
**III.5** οὕτως ἀ. **hear it** so **said**, i. e. **at first hearing**, ὡς οὕτω γ᾽ ἀκοῦσαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3b; ὥς γε οὑτωσὶ ἀκοῦσαι [prev. author] “Ly.” 216a.}}
**IV** **understand, take** in a certain sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.147a; especially in Scholl., as Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 333}}; τι ἐπί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 73.}}
**V** Astrology texts, **aspect mutually**, of signs equidistant from an equinoctial sign, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dorotheus Astrologus 189, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus Astr. 2.2}}; also, ={ὑπακούειν} (which see), {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.9.}}

192:
** ἀκρᾰσ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἀκρᾰ-σίη**,
**B** ={ἀκράτεια}, {{NT+7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus “Supp.” 2.10, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 234, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.221 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1145a16, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 544, NT.Matt.23.25, etc.; βρώσιος “Dialexeis [ca. 5th c.BC]” 1.3.}}

193:
** ἀκρατ-ής**, ές,
(κράτος) **powerless, impotent**, γῆρας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1236; παιδία 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 10}} of paralysed limbs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 4.951.22 (Epid.), 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.7.}}
**2** in Law, **invalid**, πρῆσις{{Refs|“GDI” 5653 (from Chios), compare “IG” 12(8).267.12 (from Thasos).}}
**II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **not having power** or **command over** a thing, γλώσσης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 884}}; φωνῆς, παντὸς τοῦ σώματος, ἑωυτοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.3, “Art.” 48, “Morb.” 2.6; ὀργῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.84; θυμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869a}}; ἀ. τῶν χειρῶν, of persons **with** their hands **tied**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.38}}; **intemperate in the use of** a thing, ἀφροδισίων, οἴνου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.2, “Oec.” 12.11}}; ἀ. κέρδους, τιμῆς **intemperate in pursuit of** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1147b33; with Preps., ἀ. πρὸς τὸν οἶνον [prev. author] “HA” 594a10; περὶ τὰ πόματα [prev. author] “PA” 691a3: with}}_infinitive_, ἀ. εἴργεσθαί τινος **unable to** refrain from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 252c.}}_adverb_ -τῶς, _Ionic dialect_ -τέως, διακεῖσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.)” 55.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_ in moral sense, **without command over** oneself or one's passions, **incontinent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1145b11; ἀ. στόμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 838; νηδύς 5th c.BC: Aristias Tragicus 3}}. _adverb_ ἀκρατῶς, ἔχειν πρός τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 710a.}}
**II.3** of things, **uncontrolled, immoderate**, δαπάνη{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.367 (2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista)}}; οὖρον.. ἀκρατές **incontinence** of urine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.6}}; compare ἀκρᾰτί.

194:
** ἄκρᾱτος**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἄκρητος, ον: (κεράννυμι):
**1** of liquids, **unmixed, neat**, especially of wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.73}}; ἄκρητοι σπονδαί drink-offerings **of pure wine**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.341, 4.159}}; οἶνος πάνυ ἄ. very **strong**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.27}}; οἶνος ἄκρητος wine **without water**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, etc.}}; ἄκρατος (without οἶνος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 105, etc.}}; ὁ πολὺς ἄ. ὁλίγ᾽ ἀναγκάζει φρονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 779, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 43, 3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 3.3}}; ἄκρατον, τό, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1461a15; γάλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.297; αἷμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 578, etc.(without αἷμα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.ά)}}; χυμός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14; ὑποχωρήσιες [prev. author] “Aph.” 7.6; διάρροια 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.24 (-κρίτως Littre).}}
**2** of any objects, ἄ. σώματα **pure, simple** bodies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 57c; ἄ. χρῶμα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 42}}; ἄ. μέλαν **pure** black, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Col.” 26; ἄ. νύξ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 262, compare “NA” 12.33; ἄ. σκότος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 21; ἄ. σκιά [prev. author] 2.932b.}}
**3** of qualities, **pure, absolute**, ἄ. νοῦς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.20; πῶς.. ἡ ἄ. δικαιοσύνη πρὸς ἀδικίαν τὴν ἄ. ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 545a, compare 491e}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs|“Lg.” 731d.}}
**4** of conditions or states, **pure, untempered, absolute**, ἐλευθερία, ἡδονή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“R.” 562d, “Lg.” 793a; ὀλιγαρχία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273b37, etc.; παρρησία 4th c.BC: Demades Orator 18; νόμων ἀποτομία “POxy.” 237 vii 40 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἄ. νόμος **absolute** law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 723a}}; ἄ. ψεῦδος **sheer** lie, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 382c.}}_adverb_ ἀκράτως **absolutely, entirely**, ἀ. μέλας, λευκός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.11, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 1.3.}}
**5** of persons, **intemperate, violent**, ἄ. ὀργήν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 678}}; of sleep, ἄ. ἐλθέ come **with all thy power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 602.}}
**6** of feelings, ἄ. ὀργή{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406a10; ἵμερος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941; ἄ. καῦμα “Anthologia Graeca” 9.71 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); φόβος “Etymologicum Magnum” 621.13; τὸ τῆς δεισιδαιμονίας ἄ. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.9.3, etc.}}
**II** _comparative_ ἀκρατέστερος, _Ionic dialect_ ἀκρητ- (asiffr.ἀκρατής) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 5, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator}}**Dem.fragment (b)**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 871a16, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 24}}: _superlative_ ἀκρατέστατος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 53a: but ἀκρατότερος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.677c.}}

195:
** ἀκρῑβ-εια**, ἡ,
**exactness, precision**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22, etc.; τῶν πραχθέντων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.1, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.6:}} — frequently with Preps. in _adverb_ sense, δι᾽ ἀκριβείας **with minuteness** or **precision**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184c, “Ti.” 23d, etc.; διὰ πάσης ἀ. “Lg.” 876c; εἰς τὴν ἀ. φιλοσοφεῖν “Grg.” 487c; εἰς ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1331a2; πρὸς τὴν ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 478b1:}} —ἡ ἀ. τοῦ ναυτικοῦ its **efficiency, rigid discipline**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13}}; ἀ. νόμων **strictness, severity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.40}}; περὶ τὸ διάφορον **strictness** in money matters {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31 27.11}}: _plural_, **niceties**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 504e, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.16.}}
**2** **parsimony, frugality**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 16}}; ὕδωρ δι᾽ ἀκριβείας ἐστί τινι is **scarce**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844b.}}

196:
Related to: ** ἀκρῑβ-ής**, ές,
**exact, accurate, precise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 367, etc.; σημεῖον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; δίαιτα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.4}}; τριταῖος **returning precisely** at its time, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 1.24}}; γαλήνη **complete** calm, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.25c.}}
**II** of persons, **precise, strict**, δικασταί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46; ἐπίσκοποι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762d; δεινὸς καὶ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12}}; ἀ. τοῖς ὄμμασι **sharp-** sighted, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.194}}; of arguments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 130; ἀ. μουσική 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 906, etc.}}; τὸ ἀ., ={ἀκρίβεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9; τὸ πάνυ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18: frequently in}}_adverb_ -βῶς **to a nicety, precisely**, ἀ. εἰδέναι, ἐπίστασθαι, καθορᾶν, μαθεῖν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.32, etc.; ἀ. οἶσθα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 330}}; opposed to ἁπλῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.46}}; opposed to τύπῳ (**in outline, roughly**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1104a2}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον**{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 436c, NT.Act.18.26}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 484c}}; ἀ. καὶ μόλις **with greatest difficulty**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 16:}} —also οὐκ εἰς ἀκριβὲς ἦλθες **at the right moment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 901.}}
**II.2** **in the strict sense of the word**, ὁ ἀ. ἰατρός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 342d; ὁτῷἀ. λόγῳ ἰατρός [prev. work] 341c.}}
**II.2.b** **pure-bred, genuine**, Κόλχος{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.263D.}}
**II.2.c** Astron., **true**, opposed to φαινόμενος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 4.31.}}
**II.3** **parsimonious, stingy**, ἀ. τοὺς τρόπους{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 235}}. _adverb_ -βῶς, διαιτᾶσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.32.}} —Rare except in _Attic dialect_, mostly Prose. (The sense points to ἄκρος as the first part of the word, but -ῑβης remains uncertain)

